# =========================================================================
# orchestrator/crew_runner — crew lifecycle: assemble → execute → aggregate.
#
# Origin: rust-old/src/orchestrator/crew_runner.rs (1468 lines)
#
# **PORT IN PROGRESS — this file currently holds the module's pure leaves
# only.** crew_runner is the largest module in the port and CLAUDE.md's task
# sizing says large items go in small verified bites. The split, in dependency
# order:
#
#   * bite 14 (here) — the pure leaves: `pick_best_agent`,
#     `build_system_prompt`, `strip_provider_prefix`, `select_model`,
#     `topological_sort`. No async, no I/O, no struct, and 12 of the module's
#     30 tests land on them. (`select_model` reads hoosh's catalog since ADR 030.)
#   * bite 15 — `execute_task` (crew_runner.rs:619-898), the LLM call path.
#   * bite 16 — the `CrewRunner` struct, its builders, and the three process
#     modes (`run`, `run_sequential`, `run_parallel`, `run_dag`).
#
# `ResponseCache`, `CostTracker`, `AuditChain` and `cache_key` are **re-exports
# from the hoosh crate** (`rust-old/src/llm/mod.rs:14-20`), not agnosai code, so
# each had to be resolved against the HTTP seam before the runner core could be
# written. Settled 2026-07-30:
#
#   * **Cache — nothing to build.** hoosh caches server-side automatically, keyed
#     on the whole request body. The oracle's client-side `cache_key` hashed only
#     `messages`, while `execute_task` puts the task description in
#     `request.prompt` and the agent prompt in `request.system` — neither of
#     which is in `messages` — so for a context-free task the key collapsed to
#     `{model}:{hash(model)}` and task 2 was served task 1's output. Deleting the
#     client cache removes a wrong-answer bug, not just code.
#   * **Cost — read it off the response.** hoosh 2.6.0 reports
#     `usage.cost_micro_usd` and the real serving `usage.provider`, so the port
#     reads them via `src/llm/hoosh.cyr` instead of porting hoosh's pricing table.
#     Bill on `X-Hoosh-Cache: MISS`: a hit replays the cached body verbatim,
#     cost included, and that is what the *cached* inference cost.
#   * **Audit — agnosai owns a chain.** `/v1/audit` is GET-only and hoosh's
#     `audit_record` has no metadata slot, so appends cannot be delegated. This
#     matches the Rust build, where the two chains were already
#     separate instances with separate keys. It gets its own bite before 16,
#     where `run_sequential` needs it.
#
# ## Two functions are NOT implemented here, deliberately
#
# **`topological_sort` (crew_runner.rs:1004) is a one-line delegation** to
# `scheduler::topological_sort_tasks`, which the port already has as
# `agnosai_topological_sort_tasks` (`src/orchestrator/scheduler.cyr:347`). Writing a
# second sorter would be the classic port mistake: two implementations of one
# algorithm that drift. `run_dag` in bite 16 calls the scheduler's directly.
# Its tests already live in `tests/orch_scheduler.tcyr` and are not duplicated.
#
# **`mood_adjusted_temperature` (crew_runner.rs:985) is not ported yet.** It
# takes a `bhava::traits::PersonalityProfile`, and bhava has no Cyrius port yet
# (roadmap B5) — the
# same reason `src/orchestrator/scoring.cyr`'s personality score is pinned at the
# oracle's `None` arm. It is unreachable rather than merely unported: nothing
# can supply a profile, because `AgentDefinition`'s `personality` field is not
# in the ported struct at all (`src/core/agent.cyr` has no
# `agnosai_agent_personality`). Porting the arithmetic now would mean writing
# f64 clamp code that no caller can reach and no test can honestly exercise.
# When bhava lands, this and `agnosai_personality_score` come back together.
#
# ## Three provider vocabularies that are NOT the same list
#
# This is the highest drift risk in the module, so it is written down rather
# than left for a reviewer to "tidy":
#
#   1. hoosh's **`provider_name` spellings**, which are `usage.provider` and a
#      catalog entry's `owned_by`: `openai`, `anthropic`, `groq`, `ollama` and so
#      on. What a result's `provider` says. agnosai holds no copy of the list
#      (ADR 030).
#   2. `_agnosai_crew_strip_provider_prefix`'s **twelve litellm segment names**
#      — `ollama`, `openai`, `anthropic`, `groq`, `deepseek`, `mistral`,
#      `together`, `fireworks`, `anyscale`, `perplexity`, `bedrock`, `azure`.
#      Matched case-SENSITIVELY and as a whole segment.
#   3. `agnosai_provider_to_wire` / `_from_wire` (`src/llm/hoosh.cyr`) — the
#      **wire spellings**: `open_ai`, `anthropic`, `google`, `mistral`,
#      `deep_seek`, `grok`, `ollama`. Nothing in `src/` reads these since ADR 030.
#
# The collisions that would drift: `deepseek` is one word in (1) and (2) but
# `deep_seek` in (3), and `openai` is one word in (1) and (2) but `open_ai` in
# (3). Copying (3)'s spellings into (2) makes `openai/gpt-4o` and `deepseek/x`
# stop stripping — and `openai/gpt-4o` is one of the three cases the oracle's own
# `test_select_model_strips_provider_prefix` asserts. Set (3) also carries
# `google` and `grok`, which are not strippable prefixes, and lacks six of set
# (2)'s twelve. Separately, `groq` in (2) is one letter from `grok` in (3), and
# the oracle's provider enum has no Groq variant at all.
#
# ## Two complexity vocabularies, and the asymmetry is the contract
#
# `agnosai_parse_complexity` (`src/llm/router.cyr`) takes four spellings —
# `low`/`simple` → Simple, `high`/`complex` → Complex, else Medium — while
# `agnosai_complexity_level` (`src/orchestrator/scoring.cyr`) takes two, `low` → 1,
# `high` → 3, else 2. Both mirror their own oracle function faithfully. The
# consequence looks like a bug and is not: an agent with `complexity =
# "simple"` routes as Simple for **model selection** but scores as level 2
# (medium) for **agent selection**. `select_model` must call
# `agnosai_parse_complexity` and must never call `agnosai_complexity_level`;
# pointing both at one helper would silently change agent-selection scores.
#
# ## Everything here allocates through `a`
#
# `a` is the crew's allocator (`agnosai_crew_alloc`, ADR 028): the arena a crew lives
# in, or the global heap, which is what every crew had through 2.2.4 and is what a
# crew built without an arena still has. So a bare `alloc`, `str_from`, `vec_new`
# or `bayan_json_v_*_new` on this path is a leak in an arena crew, and
# `tests/orch_crew_arena.tcyr` measures for one: a crew it runs and releases must
# leave `alloc_used()` where it found it.
# =========================================================================

include "src/done_word.cyr"
include "src/log_fields.cyr"
include "src/orchestrator/budget.cyr"
include "src/handoff.cyr"

# Pick the highest-scoring agent for a task, or 0 when the roster is empty.
#
# Returns the agent POINTER, not an index: index 0 is a real agent and in fact
# the most common winner, since `agnosai_rank_agents` breaks ties on ascending
# index. A `-1` index sentinel would work too but every call site would have to
# remember it, and 0-as-absent is the port's convention everywhere else.
#
# The oracle clones the winner; this returns the borrow. Nothing mutates an
# agent definition after a crew starts, and `rust-old/benches/orchestrator.rs`
# benchmarks this very function as `rank_agents(...).first()` taking
# `&agents[*idx]` — the clone is not semantic.
#
# **This is `agnosai_rank_agents` plus `.first()`, and reusing it is the point.**
# The oracle's body calls `scoring::score_agent` per agent, which re-extracts
# `required_tools` from the task each time; `rank_agents` hoists that extraction
# out of the loop. The extraction is a pure read of the task context, so the
# scores are bit-identical — `rank_agents` is simply the newer path that
# `pick_best_agent` was never updated to use. One visible consequence: on a
# malformed `required_tools` the oracle emits N warnings for N agents and this
# emits exactly one. Log volume only, but sakshi is an audit trail here, so it
# is said out loud rather than left to be found in a log diff.
#
# **Do not route this through `agnosai_delegate_tasks`.** That shares the same
# three-line picker core but has a different empty arm — it falls back to the
# manager, which is `hierarchical.rs`'s contract and not this one. Here an empty
# roster means no agent, and the callers turn that into the generic system
# prompt and the label "unassigned".
fn _agnosai_crew_pick_best_agent(agents, task): i64 {
    return _agnosai_crew_pick_best_agent_a(default_alloc(), agents, task);
}

# `_agnosai_crew_pick_best_agent`, in allocator `a`.
fn _agnosai_crew_pick_best_agent_a(a, agents, task): i64 {
    if (vec_len(agents) == 0) { return 0; }

    var ranked = agnosai_rank_agents_a(a, agents, task);
    if (vec_len(ranked) == 0) { return 0; }

    var best = vec_get(agents, agnosai_scored_index(vec_get(ranked, 0)));
    if (sakshi_get_level() >= SK_DEBUG) {
        var sb = str_builder_new_a(a);
        str_builder_add_a(a, sb, agnosai_agent_key(best));
        str_builder_add_cstr_a(a, sb, " task=");
        str_builder_add_a(a, sb, agnosai_task_id(task));
        var line = str_builder_build_a(a, sb);
        sakshi_log_kv(SK_DEBUG, "picked best agent for task", 26, "agent_key", 9,
          str_data(line), str_len(line));
    }
    return best;
}

# Build the system prompt from an agent definition, or the generic one when
# there is no agent.
#
# Byte-exact against the oracle. The separator between every section is `\n\n`;
# a field that is absent contributes **nothing at all**, not an empty section.
# The presence test is on the field being set, NOT on it being non-empty — an
# agent with `backstory = ""` emits `\n\nBackstory: ` with nothing after it,
# which is what the oracle's `if let Some(ref backstory)` does. The one
# exception is `tools`, which the oracle gates on `!is_empty()`, so an empty
# tool vec emits nothing while a vec holding one empty string emits
# `\n\nAvailable tools: `.
#
# `You are {name}, a {role}.` produces "a engineer" for a vowel-initial role.
# That is the oracle's output and it stays; a test pins it so nobody "fixes" it.
#
# The oracle has a fifth block after tools: when `agent.personality` is set it
# appends a single `\n` (not `\n\n`) plus `profile.compose_prompt()`. That block
# is **unreachable in this port** — bhava is deferred (roadmap.md, *What agnosai
# is* and D2; port-plan.md:219) and the ported
# `AgentDefinition` has no personality field — so it is not written here. It is
# the only part of this function that is not ported, and it comes back with
# bhava alongside `agnosai_personality_score`.
fn _agnosai_crew_build_system_prompt(agent): i64 {
    return _agnosai_crew_build_system_prompt_a(default_alloc(), agent);
}

# `_agnosai_crew_build_system_prompt`, in allocator `a`.
fn _agnosai_crew_build_system_prompt_a(a, agent): i64 {
    if (agent == 0) {
        return str_from_a(a, "You are a helpful AI assistant executing tasks within a crew.");
    }

    var sb = str_builder_new_a(a);
    str_builder_add_cstr_a(a, sb, "You are ");
    str_builder_add_a(a, sb, agnosai_agent_name(agent));
    str_builder_add_cstr_a(a, sb, ", a ");
    str_builder_add_a(a, sb, agnosai_agent_role(agent));
    str_builder_add_cstr_a(a, sb, ".\n\nGoal: ");
    str_builder_add_a(a, sb, agnosai_agent_goal(agent));

    var backstory = agnosai_agent_backstory(agent);
    if (backstory != 0) {
        str_builder_add_cstr_a(a, sb, "\n\nBackstory: ");
        str_builder_add_a(a, sb, backstory);
    }

    var domain = agnosai_agent_domain(agent);
    if (domain != 0) {
        str_builder_add_cstr_a(a, sb, "\n\nDomain expertise: ");
        str_builder_add_a(a, sb, domain);
    }

    var tools = agnosai_agent_tools(agent);
    if (vec_len(tools) > 0) {
        str_builder_add_cstr_a(a, sb, "\n\nAvailable tools: ");
        for (var i = 0; i < vec_len(tools); i = i + 1) {
            if (i > 0) { str_builder_add_cstr_a(a, sb, ", "); }
            str_builder_add_a(a, sb, vec_get(tools, i));
        }
    }

    return str_builder_build_a(a, sb);
}

# The twelve litellm provider segments the oracle strips, and only those.
#
# Case-SENSITIVE and whole-segment: `Ollama/x` and `openai-custom/x` are both
# left alone. The list is deliberately narrower than the provider enum — it has
# no `google` or `grok`, and it does have `groq`, `together`, `fireworks`,
# `anyscale`, `perplexity`, `bedrock` and `azure`, which are not enum variants.
# Do not "complete" it from `agnosai_provider_to_wire`; see the module header.
fn _agnosai_crew_is_provider_segment(seg: Str): i64 {
    if (str_eq_cstr(seg, "ollama") == 1) { return 1; }
    if (str_eq_cstr(seg, "openai") == 1) { return 1; }
    if (str_eq_cstr(seg, "anthropic") == 1) { return 1; }
    if (str_eq_cstr(seg, "groq") == 1) { return 1; }
    if (str_eq_cstr(seg, "deepseek") == 1) { return 1; }
    if (str_eq_cstr(seg, "mistral") == 1) { return 1; }
    if (str_eq_cstr(seg, "together") == 1) { return 1; }
    if (str_eq_cstr(seg, "fireworks") == 1) { return 1; }
    if (str_eq_cstr(seg, "anyscale") == 1) { return 1; }
    if (str_eq_cstr(seg, "perplexity") == 1) { return 1; }
    if (str_eq_cstr(seg, "bedrock") == 1) { return 1; }
    if (str_eq_cstr(seg, "azure") == 1) { return 1; }
    return 0;
}

# Strip a known `provider/` prefix from a model identifier.
#
# litellm-style ids use `provider/model`, but most inference endpoints — Ollama's
# OpenAI-compatible API included — want the bare model name.
#
# Returns a **view** into the input, matching the oracle's `&str` return: no
# copy, no allocation. Only the FIRST `/` is considered and only one level is
# stripped, so `ollama/openai/gpt-4o` yields `openai/gpt-4o`. A `while` loop
# here would be wrong and no oracle test would catch it.
#
# The guard is `idx < 0`, not `idx <= 0`: `/model` has its slash at index 0,
# whose prefix is the empty segment, which matches nothing and so falls through
# to returning the input unchanged. And `ollama/` yields an empty Str rather
# than the input — the oracle ships the empty remainder as-is with no
# validation and no fallback.
fn _agnosai_crew_strip_provider_prefix(model: Str): i64 {
    return _agnosai_crew_strip_provider_prefix_a(default_alloc(), model);
}

# `_agnosai_crew_strip_provider_prefix`, in allocator `a`.
fn _agnosai_crew_strip_provider_prefix_a(a, model: Str): i64 {
    var idx = str_index_of(model, 47);
    if (idx < 0) { return model; }
    if (_agnosai_crew_is_provider_segment(str_sub_a(a, model, 0, idx)) == 0) { return model; }
    return str_sub_a(a, model, idx + 1, str_len(model) - idx - 1);
}

# The id to send for a model an agent names.
#
# **A listed id goes as listed** (ADR 030). The strip is for litellm's `provider/model` spelling, and
# `provider/` is also how some providers spell a model: Groq serves `openai/gpt-oss-120b` and every
# OpenRouter id is `vendor/model`. Only the catalog can tell the two apart. An id the strip would not
# touch needs no catalog, so an agent naming `gpt-4o` causes no read at all. With no catalog, or an id
# it does not list, the strip stands, which is what such an id has always got.
fn _agnosai_crew_named_model_a(a, client, named: Str): i64 {
    var stripped = _agnosai_crew_strip_provider_prefix_a(a, named);
    if (stripped == named) { return named; }
    if (agnosai_model_catalog_listed(client, named) == 1) { return named; }
    return stripped;
}

# The tier of `agent`'s task. No agent is Capable. In `a`.
fn _agnosai_crew_tier_a(a, agent): i64 {
    if (agent == 0) { return AGNOSAI_TIER_CAPABLE; }
    var complexity = agnosai_parse_complexity(agnosai_agent_complexity(agent));
    return agnosai_route(agnosai_task_profile_new_a(a, AGNOSAI_TASKTYPE_REASON, complexity));
}

# Select the model for a task: its agent's own, else the one its tier means.
#
# Returns 0 with the id through `out_model`, or why there is none, which is a task that does not run
# rather than one that sends a guess (ADR 030). The id may be in `a` or last the process. Three arms,
# in the oracle's precedence:
#
#   1. An agent with `llm_model` set — sent as `_agnosai_crew_named_model_a` says.
#   2. An agent without one — `parse_complexity` on the agent's complexity, then the router's
#      Reason-task matrix, then that tier in the gateway's catalog.
#   3. No agent at all — the Capable tier.
#
# The presence test in arm 1 is `!= 0`, never `str_len(...) > 0`: an agent whose
# `llm_model` is explicitly `""` gets `""`, not the routed default. `str_from("")`
# is a real 16-byte header with length 0, so absent and empty are genuinely
# distinguishable here and conflating them is the likeliest silent divergence.
#
# Arm 2 goes through the full router rather than a direct complexity→tier
# table, and the reason is the counter-intuitive `low` case: Simple + Reason
# routes to **Capable**, never Fast. A shortcut table would write `low → fast`
# and pass every test that does not cover `low`. As a consequence
# `AGNOSAI_TIER_FAST` is unreachable from here at all.
fn _agnosai_crew_select_model(client, agent, out_model): i64 {
    return _agnosai_crew_select_model_a(default_alloc(), client, agent, out_model);
}

# `_agnosai_crew_select_model`, in allocator `a`, for a task that sends no tools.
fn _agnosai_crew_select_model_a(a, client, agent, out_model): i64 {
    return _agnosai_crew_select_model_tools_a(a, client, agent, 0, out_model);
}

# `_agnosai_crew_select_model_a`, for a task that sends tools when `tools` is 1: arm 2 is then never
# given a model the gateway says takes none (`agnosai_model_for_tier_tools_a`). Arm 1 is the agent's
# word and is sent as named.
fn _agnosai_crew_select_model_tools_a(a, client, agent, tools, out_model): i64 {
    if (agent != 0) {
        var named = agnosai_agent_llm_model(agent);
        if (named != 0) {
            store64(out_model, _agnosai_crew_named_model_a(a, client, named));
            return 0;
        }
    }
    return agnosai_model_for_tier_tools_a(a, client, _agnosai_crew_tier_a(a, agent), tools,
      out_model);
}

# Whether `agent`'s task sends tools: a loop to run them, and an agent that names one. 1 or 0.
fn _agnosai_crew_sends_tools(agent, loop): i64 {
    if (loop == 0 || agent == 0) { return 0; }
    if (vec_len(agnosai_agent_tools(agent)) > 0) { return 1; }
    return 0;
}

# Whether nothing a catalog says could change what `agent`'s task sends: an id the strip leaves alone,
# or a tier the operator named. A refused id of that kind is not worth asking the gateway about, and
# with an agent that names a typo, every task would.
fn _agnosai_crew_selection_fixed_a(a, agent): i64 {
    if (agent != 0) {
        var named = agnosai_agent_llm_model(agent);
        if (named != 0) {
            if (_agnosai_crew_strip_provider_prefix_a(a, named) == named) { return 1; }
            return 0;
        }
    }
    if (agnosai_model_tier_override(_agnosai_crew_tier_a(a, agent)) != 0) { return 1; }
    return 0;
}

# What to send instead of `model` after the gateway refused it with `err`, or 0 to let `err` stand.
#
# A held catalog goes stale: hoosh refreshes every six hours, a provider retires an id between two of
# them, and a process keeps its first read for good. So a refused id reads the list again, at most
# once a minute, and the task selects from what is there. The same id coming back means the list had
# no better answer, and the failure is the true one. What the agent or the operator named is theirs,
# and costs the gateway nothing, unless the strip renamed it and a read might let it go out as listed.
# `tools` is 1 for a task that sends tools, which selects again under the same rule. In `a`.
fn _agnosai_crew_reselect_a(a, client, agent, model: Str, err: Str, tools): Str {
    if (agnosai_hoosh_refused_model(err) == 0) { return 0; }
    if (_agnosai_crew_selection_fixed_a(a, agent) == 1) { return 0; }
    agnosai_model_catalog_reread(client);
    var next: i64[1];
    store64(&next, 0);
    if (_agnosai_crew_select_model_tools_a(a, client, agent, tools, &next) != 0) { return 0; }
    if (str_eq(load64(&next), model) == 1) { return 0; }
    return load64(&next);
}

# ── bite 15: execute_task ───────────────────

# The oracle's base temperature, in the port's integer thousandths.
var AGNOSAI_CREW_TEMPERATURE_MILLI = 700;

# The temperature a validation retry drops to — the oracle's 0.1.
var AGNOSAI_CREW_RETRY_TEMPERATURE_MILLI = 100;

# The oracle's `max_tokens: Some(4096)`.
var AGNOSAI_CREW_MAX_TOKENS = 4096;

# Cyrius has no closures, so `with_retry`'s callback captures become a struct.
#
# `TRACE` is the task's `invoke_agent` span context, or 0: every attempt's
# `chat` span is that span's child (ADR 023).
enum AgnosaiInferCtxOff {
    AGN_ICTX_CLIENT = 0;
    AGN_ICTX_REQUEST = 8;
    AGN_ICTX_TRACE = 16;
    AGN_ICTX_SIZE = 24;
}

# One inference attempt — the `callptr` target `agnosai_with_retry` drives.
#
# Returns 0 and stores the response through `out_value`, or an error Str. That
# is exactly `agnosai_hoosh_chat_in`'s contract, and it is what
# `agnosai_is_retryable` classifies on.
#
# The call goes through the client's chat pointer, which is the live call unless
# a suite swapped it (`agnosai_hoosh_client_with_chat`, ADR 022). A pointer of 0
# — a client not built by `agnosai_hoosh_client_new` — falls back to the live
# call rather than jumping to address 0. The span context is the pointer's
# fourth argument, so a stub sees exactly what the live call would.
fn _agnosai_crew_infer_fp(ctx, out_value): i64 {
    var client = load64(ctx + AGN_ICTX_CLIENT);
    var chat = agnosai_hoosh_client_chat_fp(client);
    if (chat == 0) { chat = &_agnosai_hoosh_client_chat_live; }
    return callptr(chat, client, load64(ctx + AGN_ICTX_REQUEST), out_value,
      load64(ctx + AGN_ICTX_TRACE));
}

# Build the retry context. `trace` is the task's span context, or 0.
fn _agnosai_crew_infer_ctx_new_a(a, client, request, trace): i64 {
    var c = alloc_via(a, AGN_ICTX_SIZE);
    store64(c + AGN_ICTX_CLIENT, client);
    store64(c + AGN_ICTX_REQUEST, request);
    store64(c + AGN_ICTX_TRACE, trace);
    return c;
}

# Log one of the oracle's task-execution events.
#
# Local rather than reusing `orch_hierarchical`'s: that would make this module
# depend on hierarchical for a log line, and the test file would have to include
# a module it does not otherwise need.
# Scan model output for leaked secrets and PII, log what it finds, and redact
# when enabled. Returns the content to use as the task result.
#
# ⚠ **This is a DELIBERATE DIVERGENCE from the oracle — see ADR 020.** The
# oracle never calls `output_filter` at all: `rust-old/src/server/mod.rs:11` is
# `pub mod output_filter;` and that is the module's ONLY mention in the entire
# Rust tree. The port reproduced that faithfully, so the module was 20 functions
# with passing tests and no caller. Compare `prompt_guard`, whose oracle DOES
# wire it (`rust-old/src/orchestrator/crew_runner.rs:651`) and which this file
# wires at four sites — the inbound half was live while the outbound half was
# not, which is the asymmetry this closes.
#
# The system prompt comes from the agent so `agnosai_contains_prompt_leak` can
# tell a genuine leak from ordinary text, exactly as the module's own signature
# asks for.
fn _agnosai_crew_filter_output(content: Str, agent, task_id: Str): i64 {
    return _agnosai_crew_filter_output_a(default_alloc(), content, agent, task_id);
}

# `_agnosai_crew_filter_output`, in allocator `a`.
fn _agnosai_crew_filter_output_a(a, content: Str, agent, task_id: Str): i64 {
    var findings = agnosai_output_scan_a(a, content,
      agnosai_wrap_system_prompt_a(a, _agnosai_crew_build_system_prompt_a(a, agent)));
    var n = vec_len(findings);
    if (n == 0) { return content; }

    if (sakshi_get_level() >= SK_WARN) {
        for (var i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) {
            var f = vec_get(findings, i);
            var sb = str_builder_new_a(a);
            str_builder_add_a(a, sb, task_id);
            str_builder_add_cstr_a(a, sb, " category=");
            str_builder_add_a(a, sb, agnosai_finding_category(f));
            str_builder_add_cstr_a(a, sb, " pattern=");
            str_builder_add_a(a, sb, agnosai_finding_pattern(f));
            var line = str_builder_build_a(a, sb);
            sakshi_log_kv(SK_WARN, "sensitive content in model output", 33,
              "task_id", 7, str_data(line), str_len(line));
        }
    }

    if (agnosai_output_redact_enabled() == 0) { return content; }
    return agnosai_output_redact_a(a, content);
}

fn _agnosai_crew_log_a(a, level, msg, msg_len, task_id: Str, agent_label: Str): i64 {
    if (sakshi_get_level() < level) { return 0; }
    var sb = str_builder_new_a(a);
    str_builder_add_a(a, sb, task_id);
    if (agent_label != 0) {
        str_builder_add_cstr_a(a, sb, " agent=");
        str_builder_add_a(a, sb, agent_label);
    }
    var line = str_builder_build_a(a, sb);
    sakshi_log_kv(level, msg, msg_len, "task_id", 7, str_data(line), str_len(line));
    return 0;
}

# The agent's key, or the oracle's `"unassigned"` when there is no agent.
fn _agnosai_crew_agent_label(agent): i64 {
    return _agnosai_crew_agent_label_a(default_alloc(), agent);
}

# `_agnosai_crew_agent_label`, in allocator `a`.
fn _agnosai_crew_agent_label_a(a, agent): i64 {
    if (agent == 0) { return str_from_a(a, "unassigned"); }
    return agnosai_agent_key(agent);
}

# The crew id a task carries in its context, or "unknown".
#
# The oracle reads `task.context["crew_id"]` rather than the spec's own id, and
# falls back to the literal `"unknown"` (`crew_runner.rs:786-801` as of tag
# 2.1.4). **This is now the STANDALONE fallback only** — ADR 022. A runner passes
# its spec's id down through `agnosai_execute_task_in_crew`, so its `token`
# events carry the crew they belong to, like every other event it emits. Only an
# `agnosai_execute_task` called outside a runner still lands here.
#
# ⚠ **Do not "fix" the runner path by stamping `crew_id` into each task's
# context.** `_agnosai_crew_build_request` renders the whole context map into
# the prompt: every context-free task would gain a context preamble, an internal
# id would reach the model, and the request body — hoosh's cache key — would
# differ per crew.
fn _agnosai_crew_event_crew_id(task): i64 {
    var ctx = agnosai_task_context(task);
    if (ctx != 0) {
        var v = map_get(ctx, str_from("crew_id"));
        if (v != 0 && bayan_json_v_is_str(v) == 1) { return bayan_json_v_str(v); }
    }
    return str_from("unknown");
}

# Milliseconds elapsed since `start_ns`.
fn _agnosai_crew_elapsed_ms(start_ns): i64 {
    return (clock_now_ns() - start_ns) / AGNOSAI_NS_PER_MS;
}

# The task's own prompt: the sanitized description, plus the sanitized expected
# output after a blank line when the task has one.
#
# This is what a validation retry is anchored to, which is why it is apart from
# `_agnosai_crew_user_message`: `agnosai_build_retry_prompt` ends with the schema
# itself, and an original that carried it too would say it twice.
fn _agnosai_crew_task_prompt_a(a, task): i64 {
    var msg = agnosai_prompt_sanitize_a(a, agnosai_task_description(task),
      str_from_a(a, "task_description"));
    var expected = agnosai_task_expected_output(task);
    if (expected == 0) { return msg; }

    var sb = str_builder_new_a(a);
    str_builder_add_a(a, sb, msg);
    str_builder_add_cstr_a(a, sb, "\n\n");
    str_builder_add_a(a, sb, agnosai_prompt_sanitize_a(a, expected,
        str_from_a(a, "expected_output")));
    return str_builder_build_a(a, sb);
}

# Assemble the first user message: the task's prompt, then its `output_schema`
# when it declares one.
#
# ⚠ **The schema is in the first prompt (2.2.5).** It used to reach the model only
# inside `agnosai_build_retry_prompt`, after a failure, so unless a task's text
# happened to describe the shape, every task with a schema paid for a call that
# could not have passed. The oracle has the same gap. A consumer's schema is
# request input like `expected_output`, so it is sanitized the same way, and the
# line that leads it in is ours and stays outside the markers.
fn _agnosai_crew_user_message(task): i64 {
    return _agnosai_crew_user_message_a(default_alloc(), task);
}

# `_agnosai_crew_user_message`, in allocator `a`.
fn _agnosai_crew_user_message_a(a, task): i64 {
    var msg = _agnosai_crew_task_prompt_a(a, task);
    var schema = agnosai_task_output_schema(task);
    if (schema == 0) { return msg; }

    var sb = str_builder_new_a(a);
    str_builder_add_a(a, sb, msg);
    str_builder_add_cstr_a(a, sb, "\n\nRespond with ONLY valid JSON matching this schema:\n");
    str_builder_add_a(a, sb, agnosai_prompt_sanitize_a(a, bayan_json_v_build_a(a, schema),
        str_from_a(a, "output_schema")));
    return str_builder_build_a(a, sb);
}

# Build the inference request for a task.
#
# **The port folds the system prompt into `messages` itself.** The oracle's
# `InferenceRequest` had separate `prompt` / `system` / `messages` fields and
# relied on the Rust hoosh client to merge them at the wire; the port's request
# carries only `messages`, so the fold happens here — system first, then the
# context preamble pair, then the user turn.
#
# Temperature is the flat base: `mood_adjusted_temperature` needs a bhava
# profile and the ported `AgentDefinition` has no personality field, so the
# oracle's adjustment arm is unreachable. See the module header.
#
# `user_msg` of 0 means "derive it from the task"; a validation retry passes the
# rebuilt prompt instead. Rebuilding the whole request per retry rather than
# mutating one is what keeps the retry prompt anchored to the ORIGINAL — the
# oracle's own guard against exponential prompt growth.
# A small pool of arenas for the per-task context tree, created on first use.
#
# ⚠ Lazily, and pooled rather than per-call: an arena costs its capacity
# permanently on the no-free bump, so minting one per task would cost more than
# the tree it reclaims — the lesson the audit scratch learned
# (`AGN_CR_SCRATCH`). Pooled arenas are mutex-guarded, so the parallel and DAG
# workers can share them, which the audit scratch deliberately cannot.
# ⚠ **Built EAGERLY, at module scope.** `src/arena_pool.cyr`'s header states the
# rule and this code broke it: "Build the pool EAGERLY on the main thread. A lazy
# `if (pool == 0)` double constructs when two spawned threads race it, and the
# loser's arenas leak — the very thing this module exists to stop."
# `_agnosai_crew_run_parallel` spawns a worker per task and every one of them
# reaches `_agnosai_crew_build_request`, so the race is the common case on a
# crew's first wave, not a corner. `_AGNOSAI_AUDIT_POOL` in
# `tools/builtin/security_audit.cyr` is the pattern to copy.
var _AGNOSAI_CREW_CTX_POOL = agnosai_arena_pool_new(4, 8192);

fn _agnosai_crew_build_request(task, agent, model: Str, user_msg: Str): i64 {
    return _agnosai_crew_build_request_a(default_alloc(), task, agent, model, user_msg);
}

# `_agnosai_crew_build_request`, in allocator `a`.
fn _agnosai_crew_build_request_a(a, task, agent, model: Str, user_msg: Str): i64 {
    var req = agnosai_inference_request_new_a(a, model);

    agnosai_inference_request_say(req, AGNOSAI_ROLE_SYSTEM,
      agnosai_wrap_system_prompt_a(a, _agnosai_crew_build_system_prompt_a(a, agent)));

    # The context preamble, when the task has one. The oracle serialises the
    # context map with `to_string_pretty` — the same 2-space form
    # `bayan_json_v_build_pretty` produces.
    var ctx = agnosai_task_context(task);
    if (ctx != 0 && map_size(ctx) > 0) {
        # ⚠ **The one genuinely transient JSON tree in this module** (roadmap
        # B2's tail). Of the 67 `bayan_json_v_*` calls here, 16 build event
        # payloads a subscriber reads later, 14 build task-result metadata and 6
        # build the profile — all RETAINED in the crew state, so none can move to
        # an arena. This object is different: it exists only to be handed to
        # `bayan_json_v_build_pretty`, and nothing downstream holds it.
        #
        # Measured at **240 B/task** for a task carrying three context keys
        # (960 B over a 4-task run, 8,504 -> 9,464). The rendered string is built
        # by the non-`_a` `build_pretty`, so it lands on the global bump and
        # outlives the reset — only the tree is reclaimed.
        # ⚠ **0 means "no arena" — it must NOT fall back to `default_alloc()`.**
        # An earlier version did, and the release below then handed the GLOBAL
        # allocator to `agnosai_arena_pool_release`, whose `reset_via` is
        # `_bump_reset` -> `alloc_reset()`: that zeroes and rewinds the whole
        # process heap, while every other pooled worker still holds pointers into
        # it. A degradation under memory pressure became whole-process memory
        # corruption. Every other `agnosai_arena_pool_acquire` site in `src/`
        # bails on 0; this one now does too, falling back to the global bump for
        # the tree without ever releasing it.
        var ctx_arena = agnosai_arena_pool_acquire(_AGNOSAI_CREW_CTX_POOL);
        var ctx_alloc = ctx_arena;
        if (ctx_alloc == 0) { ctx_alloc = default_alloc(); }
        # ⚠ Slot-walked, NOT `map_keys` — standing trap #2 under roadmap B2.
        # `map_keys` builds its key vec with `vec_new()` on the no-free global
        # bump and has no `_a` form, so it leaks per task and silently caps any
        # threading done around it. The walk also drops the second hash lookup:
        # `map_get(ctx, key)` per key became `_agnosai_map_slot_val` on the slot
        # already in hand.
        var obj = bayan_json_v_obj_new_a(ctx_alloc);
        var slots = _agnosai_map_slots(ctx);
        for (var i = 0; i < slots; i = i + 1) {
            if (_agnosai_map_slot_live(ctx, i) == 1) {
                bayan_json_v_obj_set_a(ctx_alloc, obj, _agnosai_map_slot_key(ctx, i),
                  _agnosai_map_slot_val(ctx, i));
            }
        }
        var rendered = bayan_json_v_build_pretty_a(a, obj, 2);
        if (ctx_arena != 0) {
            agnosai_arena_pool_release(_AGNOSAI_CREW_CTX_POOL, ctx_arena);
        }
        agnosai_inference_request_say(req, AGNOSAI_ROLE_USER,
          agnosai_prompt_sanitize_a(a, rendered, str_from_a(a, "context")));
        agnosai_inference_request_say(req, AGNOSAI_ROLE_ASSISTANT,
          str_from_a(a, "Understood, I have the context."));
    }

    # What the task's dependencies answered (ADR 034), already in its markers, and a reply, so the
    # task's own turn still comes last. A validation retry rebuilds the request with it, as it does
    # with the context.
    var handoff = agnosai_task_handoff(task);
    if (handoff != 0) {
        agnosai_inference_request_say(req, AGNOSAI_ROLE_USER, handoff);
        agnosai_inference_request_say(req, AGNOSAI_ROLE_ASSISTANT, agnosai_handoff_ack_a(a));
    }

    var turn = user_msg;
    if (turn == 0) { turn = _agnosai_crew_user_message_a(a, task); }
    agnosai_inference_request_say(req, AGNOSAI_ROLE_USER, turn);
    agnosai_inference_request_with_max_tokens(req, AGNOSAI_CREW_MAX_TOKENS);
    agnosai_inference_request_with_temperature_milli(req, AGNOSAI_CREW_TEMPERATURE_MILLI);
    return req;
}

# The placeholder result — the oracle's no-LLM-client arm.
#
# Output is the task description **verbatim**, status Completed, and metadata
# carrying exactly `task_duration_ms`. Two oracle tests pin this shape.
fn _agnosai_crew_placeholder_result_a(a, task, agent, start_ns): i64 {
    # ⚠ The placeholder ECHOES the task description, which is request input — so
    # a key a user pasted into a description lands in crew output and the audit
    # trail. It goes through the same filter as model output for that reason.
    # ⚠ Filtered ONLY when redaction is enabled, unlike the LLM path which always
    # scans. There is no model output here — the placeholder echoes the task
    # description, which `prompt_guard` already sanitised on the way in — so the
    # only reason to touch it is the operator's opt-in data-destruction policy.
    # Measured: an unconditional scan costs **16.7 us** against a ~40 us/task
    # crew path, i.e. +90% on `run_crew_10_tasks_sequential`. Paying that on a
    # path with nothing to protect is not a trade worth making; the LLM path is
    # network-dominated, so the same scan is noise there.
    var placeholder = agnosai_task_description(task);
    if (agnosai_output_redact_enabled() == 1) {
        placeholder = _agnosai_crew_filter_output_a(a, placeholder, agent,
          agnosai_task_id(task));
    }
    var r = agnosai_task_result_new_a(a, agnosai_task_id(task), placeholder,
      AGNOSAI_STATUS_COMPLETED);
    agnosai_task_result_with_metadata_a(a, r, str_from_a(a, "task_duration_ms"),
      bayan_json_v_int_new_a(a, _agnosai_crew_elapsed_ms(start_ns)));
    return r;
}

# Emit the single `token` event the oracle sends on success.
#
# The oracle sends the whole completed text as one token event rather than
# streaming, deliberately: a second streaming inference would double latency and
# cost for text it already has.
#
# `crew_id` is the runner's spec id, or 0 outside a runner. **A given id wins
# over `task.context["crew_id"]`** (ADR 022): the event is delivered on that
# crew's channel, and the context is client-supplied request data that could
# name any crew at all. 0 keeps the oracle's fallback.
fn _agnosai_crew_emit_token(event_tx, task, crew_id: Str, text: Str): i64 {
    return _agnosai_crew_emit_token_a(default_alloc(), event_tx, task, crew_id, text);
}

# `_agnosai_crew_emit_token`, in allocator `a`.
fn _agnosai_crew_emit_token_a(a, event_tx, task, crew_id: Str, text: Str): i64 {
    if (event_tx == 0) { return 0; }
    var id = crew_id;
    if (id == 0) { id = _agnosai_crew_event_crew_id(task); }
    var data = bayan_json_v_obj_new_a(a);
    bayan_json_v_obj_set_a(a, data, str_from_a(a, "task_id"),
      bayan_json_v_str_new_a(a, agnosai_task_id(task)));
    bayan_json_v_obj_set_a(a, data, str_from_a(a, "token"), bayan_json_v_str_new_a(a, text));
    bayan_json_v_obj_set_a(a, data, str_from_a(a, "complete"), bayan_json_v_bool_new_a(a, 1));
    return agnosai_event_sender_send(event_tx,
      agnosai_crew_event_new_a(a, id, str_from_a(a, "token"), data));
}

# Build the metadata of a result the model answered.
#
# **`cost_micro_usd`, not `cost_usd`.** The oracle emitted an f64 dollar amount
# from its in-process cost tracker; the port reports the integer micro-USD hoosh
# returns on the response. Emitting a micro value under the old key would
# multiply every consumer's dashboard by 1e6, so the key is renamed — a
# deliberate breaking change, recorded in the CHANGELOG.
#
# The key is **omitted entirely** when the gateway did not report a cost (a
# pre-2.6.0 hoosh), rather than defaulting to 0: zero is a real cost for a local
# model, so a fabricated zero would be indistinguishable from a free inference.
#
# **`agent` is the assigned agent's key** (ADR 022), omitted for an unassigned
# task. The oracle's profile aggregates `agent_cost_usd` from exactly this key
# (`crew_runner.rs:186-194` as of tag 2.1.4) and its success arm never wrote it
# (`:820-841`), so the field was always empty there and here. Writing it
# completes the design the oracle declared. Only a result the model answered
# carries it, which since 2.2.5 includes one that never met its schema: those calls
# were made and paid for. The placeholder and error results keep the metadata
# shapes the oracle's tests pin, and neither has a cost to attribute.
fn _agnosai_crew_answer_metadata(r, resp, start_ns, agent): i64 {
    return _agnosai_crew_answer_metadata_a(default_alloc(), r, resp, start_ns, agent);
}

# `_agnosai_crew_answer_metadata`, in allocator `a`.
fn _agnosai_crew_answer_metadata_a(a, r, resp, start_ns, agent): i64 {
    agnosai_task_result_with_metadata_a(a, r, str_from_a(a, "model"),
      bayan_json_v_str_new_a(a, agnosai_inference_response_model(resp)));
    if (agent != 0) {
        agnosai_task_result_with_metadata_a(a, r, str_from_a(a, "agent"),
          bayan_json_v_str_new_a(a, agnosai_agent_key(agent)));
    }

    # Omitted when nobody knows, as `cost_micro_usd` is, and for the same reason: a name that was
    # guessed cannot be told from one that was reported.
    var provider = agnosai_inference_response_provider(resp);
    if (provider != 0) {
        agnosai_task_result_with_metadata_a(a, r, str_from_a(a, "provider"),
          bayan_json_v_str_new_a(a, provider));
    }

    # The model call that produced this answer, as `tokens` below are.
    agnosai_task_result_with_metadata_a(a, r, str_from_a(a, "latency_ms"),
      bayan_json_v_int_new_a(a, agnosai_inference_response_latency_ms(resp)));

    var tokens = bayan_json_v_obj_new_a(a);
    bayan_json_v_obj_set_a(a, tokens, str_from_a(a, "prompt"),
      bayan_json_v_int_new_a(a, agnosai_inference_response_prompt_tokens(resp)));
    bayan_json_v_obj_set_a(a, tokens, str_from_a(a, "completion"),
      bayan_json_v_int_new_a(a, agnosai_inference_response_completion_tokens(resp)));
    bayan_json_v_obj_set_a(a, tokens, str_from_a(a, "total"),
      bayan_json_v_int_new_a(a, agnosai_inference_response_total_tokens(resp)));
    agnosai_task_result_with_metadata_a(a, r, str_from_a(a, "tokens"), tokens);

    var cost = agnosai_inference_response_cost_micro_usd(resp);
    if (cost != AGNOSAI_NO_LIMIT) {
        agnosai_task_result_with_metadata_a(a, r, str_from_a(a, "cost_micro_usd"),
          bayan_json_v_int_new_a(a, cost));
    }

    agnosai_task_result_with_metadata_a(a, r, str_from_a(a, "task_duration_ms"),
      bayan_json_v_int_new_a(a, _agnosai_crew_elapsed_ms(start_ns)));
    return r;
}

# Name the provider of a response the gateway did not name one on: the catalog entry's `owned_by`.
#
# Only a hoosh before 2.6.0 leaves this to do, and a gateway's own word is better than this one:
# `owned_by` is the first route that matches, which cannot see a DLP re-route to a local provider.
# The id asked for is tried second, as a provider answers with the dated form of an alias.
fn _agnosai_crew_attribute(client, resp, model: Str): i64 {
    if (agnosai_inference_response_provider(resp) != 0) { return 0; }
    var owner = agnosai_model_catalog_owner(client, agnosai_inference_response_model(resp));
    if (owner == 0) { owner = agnosai_model_catalog_owner(client, model); }
    if (owner != 0) { agnosai_inference_response_with_provider(resp, owner); }
    return 0;
}

# The failure result — status Failed, output `"LLM error: " + err`, and metadata
# carrying exactly `error` and `task_duration_ms`.
fn _agnosai_crew_error_result(task, err: Str, start_ns): i64 {
    return _agnosai_crew_error_result_a(default_alloc(), task, err, start_ns);
}

# `_agnosai_crew_error_result`, in allocator `a`.
fn _agnosai_crew_error_result_a(a, task, err: Str, start_ns): i64 {
    var sb = str_builder_new_a(a);
    str_builder_add_cstr_a(a, sb, "LLM error: ");
    str_builder_add_a(a, sb, err);

    var r = agnosai_task_result_new_a(a, agnosai_task_id(task), str_builder_build_a(a, sb),
      AGNOSAI_STATUS_FAILED);
    agnosai_task_result_with_metadata_a(a, r, str_from_a(a, "error"),
      bayan_json_v_str_new_a(a, err));
    agnosai_task_result_with_metadata_a(a, r, str_from_a(a, "task_duration_ms"),
      bayan_json_v_int_new_a(a, _agnosai_crew_elapsed_ms(start_ns)));
    return r;
}

# The result of a task whose answers never met its `output_schema`: status Failed,
# what the model last said as `output`, and `error` saying why.
#
# ⚠ **Failed, not Completed (2.2.5).** The loop used to end and the task to read
# `completed` whatever came back, so a consumer that declared a shape was told the
# work was done about an answer that did not have it. A DAG's dependents ran
# against that output, too. A failed task stops them, which is the point of a
# failure being sticky (`_agnosai_crew_run_dag`).
#
# `output` stays the model's text and not a message, so a caller can see what it
# said, and it goes through the same filter as any other answer. No `token` event
# is emitted: that event is a finished task's output, and this is not one.
#
# `validation_attempts` counts every call that was checked, so the first counts.
fn _agnosai_crew_invalid_result_a(a, task, content: Str, verr: Str, attempts, resp, start_ns,
  agent): i64 {
    var sb = str_builder_new_a(a);
    str_builder_add_cstr_a(a, sb, "output failed validation: ");
    str_builder_add_a(a, sb, verr);

    var r = agnosai_task_result_new_a(a, agnosai_task_id(task), content, AGNOSAI_STATUS_FAILED);
    agnosai_task_result_with_metadata_a(a, r, str_from_a(a, "error"),
      bayan_json_v_str_new_a(a, str_builder_build_a(a, sb)));
    agnosai_task_result_with_metadata_a(a, r, str_from_a(a, "validation_attempts"),
      bayan_json_v_int_new_a(a, attempts));
    return _agnosai_crew_answer_metadata_a(a, r, resp, start_ns, agent);
}

# The default retry policy every task shares: it is read and never written, and
# minting one per task is 32 bytes for good on the global heap.
var _AGNOSAI_CREW_RETRY = agnosai_retry_config_default();

# The result of a task its tools stopped: status Failed, the reason as `output` and as `error`.
#
# Not `_agnosai_crew_error_result_a`, which says "LLM error: ": the gateway did nothing wrong here.
# Failed before any call, for a tool no registry holds. Failed after some, at the step limit or a
# cancel, and then the caller adds what those calls cost, as for any other failure.
fn _agnosai_crew_tool_failed_result_a(a, task, why: Str, start_ns): i64 {
    var r = agnosai_task_result_new_a(a, agnosai_task_id(task), why, AGNOSAI_STATUS_FAILED);
    agnosai_task_result_with_metadata_a(a, r, str_from_a(a, "error"),
      bayan_json_v_str_new_a(a, why));
    agnosai_task_result_with_metadata_a(a, r, str_from_a(a, "task_duration_ms"),
      bayan_json_v_int_new_a(a, _agnosai_crew_elapsed_ms(start_ns)));
    return r;
}

# Ask the model, and for as long as it answers with tool calls, take the turn and ask again.
#
# `st` is the task's conversation with its tools (ADR 029), or 0, which is one call. Returns 0 with
# the last response through `out_resp`, or an error Str, which leaves the last answer there: those
# calls were paid for. A task's tokens and cost count every call, as a validation retry's do.
#
# An attempt `agnosai_with_retry_a` repeats is only the chat call. A tool already ran once its
# result is in the request, and nothing here runs one again.
#
# `budget` is the task's tracker, or 0 (ADR 033): every call is checked against it, and what each
# costs is recorded in it, before its usage is carried onto the next.
fn _agnosai_crew_converse_a(ra, task_id: Str, client, request, trace, st, budget, out_resp): i64 {
    var err = _agnosai_crew_infer_within_a(ra, task_id, client, request, trace, budget, out_resp);
    if (st == 0) { return err; }
    while (err == 0) {
        var resp = load64(out_resp);
        if (agnosai_inference_response_tool_calls(resp) == 0) { return 0; }
        var stop = _agnosai_tool_turn_a(st, request, resp);
        if (stop != 0) { return stop; }
        err = _agnosai_crew_infer_within_a(ra, task_id, client, request, trace, budget, out_resp);
        if (err == 0) { agnosai_inference_response_carry_usage(load64(out_resp), resp); }
    }
    return err;
}

# One chat call, with its retries, if `budget` allows it (ADR 033), and what it cost recorded in
# `budget` and every tracker above it. Returns 0, or why not.
#
# ⚠ **Checked here, outside `agnosai_with_retry_a`, and that is load-bearing.** The retry wrapper
# repeats any error whose text holds `429`, `502` or `503`, and a refusal names what was used:
# `crew token budget exceeded: 4291 used` would be tried again three times. A failed attempt
# records nothing, so a check between retries could not answer differently from this one.
fn _agnosai_crew_infer_within_a(ra, task_id: Str, client, request, trace, budget, out_resp): i64 {
    if (budget != 0) {
        var at: i64[1];
        var kind = agnosai_budget_tracker_breach(budget, &at);
        if (kind != AGNOSAI_BUDGET_WITHIN) {
            var why = agnosai_budget_exceeded_message_a(ra, load64(&at), kind);
            if (sakshi_get_level() >= SK_WARN) {
                var f[AGNOSAI_LOG_FIELDS_BYTES];
                var n = agnosai_log_fields_start(&f, task_id);
                n = agnosai_log_field_str(&f, n, "reason", why);
                sakshi_log_kv(SK_WARN, "model call refused: a budget is spent", 37, "task_id", 7, &f, n);
            }
            return why;
        }
    }
    var err = agnosai_with_retry_a(ra, _AGNOSAI_CREW_RETRY, task_id, &_agnosai_crew_infer_fp,
      _agnosai_crew_infer_ctx_new_a(ra, client, request, trace), out_resp);
    if (err == 0 && budget != 0) {
        var resp = load64(out_resp);
        agnosai_budget_tracker_record_tokens(budget, agnosai_inference_response_total_tokens(resp));
        var cost = agnosai_inference_response_cost_micro_usd(resp);
        if (cost > 0) { agnosai_budget_tracker_record_cost(budget, cost); }
    }
    return err;
}

# Answer `task` with `client`, and return its result.
#
# `a` is what the result lives in, and `ra` the conversation: the same allocator for a task with
# no tools, and a turn's arena for one with (`src/tool_loop.cyr`). ⚠ **So nothing that reaches the
# result may point into `ra`.** The final response and the error are copied out below, and that is
# the only place a copy is made: with no tools the two are one allocator, and no byte of this path
# is any different from what it was.
fn _agnosai_crew_answer_a(a, ra, task, agent, client, event_tx, crew_id: Str, span, self_sc,
  model: Str, st, budget, start_ns): i64 {
    var task_id = agnosai_task_id(task);
    var request = _agnosai_crew_build_request_a(ra, task, agent, model, 0);
    if (st != 0) {
        agnosai_inference_request_with_tools(request, _agnosai_tool_task_definitions(st));
    }

    var resp: i64[1];
    store64(&resp, 0);
    var err = _agnosai_crew_converse_a(ra, task_id, client, request, self_sc, st, budget, &resp);

    # A refused id, once. ⚠ Only with no answer yet, which is what says no tool has run: this builds
    # the request again, and a task that has called a tool keeps its transcript (below).
    if (err != 0 && load64(&resp) == 0) {
        var sends_tools = 0;
        if (st != 0) { sends_tools = 1; }
        var better = _agnosai_crew_reselect_a(ra, client, agent, model, err, sends_tools);
        if (better != 0) {
            model = better;
            # In `a`, as `ra` may be gone by the time the span is recorded. A no-op at span 0.
            if (span != 0) { agnosai_genai_span_set_request_model(span, str_clone_a(a, model)); }
            request = _agnosai_crew_build_request_a(ra, task, agent, model, 0);
            if (st != 0) {
                agnosai_inference_request_with_tools(request, _agnosai_tool_task_definitions(st));
            }
            err = _agnosai_crew_converse_a(ra, task_id, client, request, self_sc, st, budget, &resp);
        }
    }

    # The output-schema validation loop. Every answer is checked, the last one
    # included: `AGNOSAI_MAX_VALIDATION_RETRIES` retries are each followed by a
    # check. Through 2.2.4 the check ran before each retry, so the last answer was
    # never read at all. A retry rebuilds its prompt from the ORIGINAL, never the
    # accumulated one, or the prompt would grow exponentially, and drops the
    # temperature to 0.1.
    #
    # `verr` is 0 when the answer passed, and holds the reason when none did.
    # `extracted` is the text that was validated, so a fenced answer that passes
    # is returned without its fence, and a consumer that parses `output` need not
    # strip it again.
    #
    # ⚠ **A task that has called a tool keeps its transcript through a retry** (ADR 029). Rebuilt,
    # the model would lose what its tools said and have to run them again, with whatever effect
    # that has. The retry prompt goes on the end instead. Each adds one failed answer, so the
    # transcript grows by `AGNOSAI_MAX_VALIDATION_RETRIES` of them at most, and never doubles.
    var schema = agnosai_task_output_schema(task);
    var verr = 0;
    var attempts = 1;
    var extracted: i64[1];
    store64(&extracted, 0);
    if (schema != 0 && err == 0) {
        var original_prompt = _agnosai_crew_task_prompt_a(ra, task);
        # Hoisted out of the retry loop — a constant minted per attempt.
        var k_failed_output = str_from_a(ra, "failed_output");
        while (err == 0) {
            verr = agnosai_extract_and_validate_a(ra,
              agnosai_inference_response_content(load64(&resp)), schema, &extracted);
            if (verr == 0 || attempts > AGNOSAI_MAX_VALIDATION_RETRIES) { break; }

            agnosai_validation_log_retry_a(ra, task_id, attempts, verr);

            # The failed output is sanitized before it goes back to the model —
            # it is model output being fed to a model, which is an injection
            # vector the oracle closes here.
            #
            # With tools it is unmarked first: a model repeating a page repeats its `</user_input>`
            # too, and this goes in a user turn, not a tool's.
            var failed_text = load64(&extracted);
            if (st != 0) { failed_text = _agnosai_tool_unmark_a(ra, failed_text); }
            var retry_prompt = agnosai_build_retry_prompt_a(ra, original_prompt,
              agnosai_prompt_sanitize_a(ra, failed_text, k_failed_output), verr, schema);

            var called = 0;
            if (st != 0) { if (_agnosai_tool_task_steps(st) > 0) { called = 1; } }
            if (called == 1) {
                agnosai_inference_request_say(request, AGNOSAI_ROLE_USER, retry_prompt);
            } else {
                request = _agnosai_crew_build_request_a(ra, task, agent, model, retry_prompt);
                if (st != 0) {
                    agnosai_inference_request_with_tools(request, _agnosai_tool_task_definitions(st));
                }
            }
            agnosai_inference_request_with_temperature_milli(request,
              AGNOSAI_CREW_RETRY_TEMPERATURE_MILLI);

            var prior = load64(&resp);
            err = _agnosai_crew_converse_a(ra, task_id, client, request, self_sc, st, budget, &resp);
            attempts = attempts + 1;
            # By what it holds and not by `err`: a retry that called a tool and then failed has a
            # newer answer in `resp`, and the older calls were paid for too.
            if (load64(&resp) != prior) {
                agnosai_inference_response_carry_usage(load64(&resp), prior);
            }
        }
    }

    if (ra != a) {
        if (load64(&resp) != 0) {
            store64(&resp, agnosai_inference_response_clone_a(a, load64(&resp)));
        }
        if (err != 0) { err = str_clone_a(a, err); }
        if (load64(&extracted) != 0) { store64(&extracted, str_clone_a(a, load64(&extracted))); }
    }
    if (load64(&resp) != 0) { _agnosai_crew_attribute(client, load64(&resp), model); }

    if (err != 0) {
        var failed = 0;
        var stopped = 0;
        if (st != 0) { stopped = _agnosai_tool_task_stopped(st); }
        if (stopped == 1) { failed = _agnosai_crew_tool_failed_result_a(a, task, err, start_ns); }
        else { failed = _agnosai_crew_error_result_a(a, task, err, start_ns); }
        # A retry that failed leaves the last answer in `resp`, and those calls were paid
        # for. A first call that failed leaves 0, and the shape the oracle's tests pin.
        if (load64(&resp) != 0) {
            _agnosai_crew_answer_metadata_a(a, failed, load64(&resp), start_ns, agent);
        }
        return failed;
    }

    var response = load64(&resp);
    var answer = agnosai_inference_response_content(response);
    if (schema != 0 && verr == 0) { answer = load64(&extracted); }
    var content = _agnosai_crew_filter_output_a(a, answer, agent, task_id);
    if (verr != 0) {
        _agnosai_crew_log_a(a, SK_WARN, "task output never met its schema", 32, task_id,
          _agnosai_crew_agent_label_a(a, agent));
        return _agnosai_crew_invalid_result_a(a, task, content, verr, attempts, response,
          start_ns, agent);
    }
    _agnosai_crew_emit_token_a(a, event_tx, task, crew_id, content);

    var result = agnosai_task_result_new_a(a, task_id, content, AGNOSAI_STATUS_COMPLETED);
    return _agnosai_crew_answer_metadata_a(a, result, response, start_ns, agent);
}

# `_agnosai_crew_answer_a` for a task whose agent holds tools, in an arena of its own.
#
# The arena is given back on the one way out. Pool empty and nothing to mint leaves `ra` the crew's
# allocator, which costs a global-heap crew its transcript and nothing else.
fn _agnosai_crew_answer_with_tools_a(a, task, agent, client, event_tx, crew_id: Str, span, self_sc,
  model: Str, loop, budget, start_ns): i64 {
    var arena = _agnosai_tool_arena_acquire();
    var ra = arena;
    if (ra == 0) { ra = a; }

    var miss: i64[1];
    var st = _agnosai_tool_task_new_a(a, ra, loop, task, agent, self_sc, &miss);
    if (st != 0) { _agnosai_tool_task_with_events(st, event_tx); }
    var result = 0;
    if (st == 0) {
        _agnosai_crew_log_a(a, SK_WARN, "task refused: its agent names a tool it cannot have", 51,
          agnosai_task_id(task), load64(&miss));
        result = _agnosai_crew_tool_failed_result_a(a, task, load64(&miss), start_ns);
    } else {
        result = _agnosai_crew_answer_a(a, ra, task, agent, client, event_tx, crew_id, span,
          self_sc, model, st, budget, start_ns);
        _agnosai_tool_task_finish_a(st, result);
    }

    if (arena != 0) { _agnosai_tool_arena_release(arena); }
    return result;
}

# The body of `agnosai_execute_task_with_tools_a`. `span` is the task's
# `invoke_agent` span and `self_sc` its context — both 0 with telemetry off.
fn _agnosai_execute_task_body_a(a, task, agent, client, event_tx, crew_id: Str, span,
  self_sc, loop, budget): i64 {
    var start_ns = clock_now_ns();
    var task_id = agnosai_task_id(task);

    if (client == 0) {
        _agnosai_crew_log_a(a, SK_DEBUG, "executing task (placeholder - no LLM client)", 42,
          task_id, _agnosai_crew_agent_label_a(a, agent));
        return _agnosai_crew_placeholder_result_a(a, task, agent, start_ns);
    }

    _agnosai_crew_log_a(a, SK_DEBUG, "executing task via LLM", 22, task_id,
      _agnosai_crew_agent_label_a(a, agent));

    var selected: i64[1];
    store64(&selected, 0);
    var unselected = _agnosai_crew_select_model_tools_a(a, client, agent,
      _agnosai_crew_sends_tools(agent, loop), &selected);
    if (unselected != 0) {
        _agnosai_crew_log_a(a, SK_WARN, "task refused: no model to send", 30, task_id,
          _agnosai_crew_agent_label_a(a, agent));
        return _agnosai_crew_error_result_a(a, task, unselected, start_ns);
    }
    var model = load64(&selected);
    # The agent has one configured model, so the span says which (the pinned
    # convention's `gen_ai.request.model` on `invoke_agent`). A no-op at span 0.
    agnosai_genai_span_set_request_model(span, model);

    # The task's own tracker when it has limits, under the crew's; else the crew's (ADR 033).
    var bt = budget;
    if (agnosai_task_max_tokens(task) != AGNOSAI_NO_LIMIT
      || agnosai_task_max_cost_micro_usd(task) != AGNOSAI_NO_LIMIT) {
        bt = agnosai_budget_tracker_limits_a(a, agnosai_task_max_tokens(task),
          agnosai_task_max_cost_micro_usd(task), budget, "task");
    }

    if (_agnosai_crew_sends_tools(agent, loop) == 1) {
        return _agnosai_crew_answer_with_tools_a(a, task, agent, client, event_tx, crew_id, span,
          self_sc, model, loop, bt, start_ns);
    }
    return _agnosai_crew_answer_a(a, a, task, agent, client, event_tx, crew_id, span, self_sc,
      model, 0, bt, start_ns);
}

# Execute one task of a crew via the LLM, or return the placeholder when there
# is no client.
#
# `client == 0` selects the placeholder arm (the oracle's `llm: None`);
# `event_tx == 0` means nobody is subscribed. Returns a TaskResult, never 0 —
# a failed inference is a `Failed` result, not an error return.
#
# `crew_id` is the spec's id, which the `token` event carries (ADR 022); the
# runner passes it on every path. 0 means "no crew" and takes the standalone
# fallback in `_agnosai_crew_event_crew_id` — that is `agnosai_execute_task`.
#
# `parent_sc` is the crew's `invoke_workflow` span context, or 0 (ADR 023).
# With telemetry on the task is an `invoke_agent` span under it, and every chat
# attempt is that span's child. The placeholder arm is recorded too: the task
# ran, and a trace with a hole where it ran would mislead.
#
# **No cache lookup.** The oracle checked an in-process `ResponseCache` here;
# hoosh caches server-side on the whole request body, which is both automatic
# and correct where the oracle's client-side key was not. See the module header.
fn agnosai_execute_task_in_crew(task, agent, client, event_tx, crew_id: Str, parent_sc): i64 {
    return agnosai_execute_task_in_crew_a(default_alloc(), task, agent, client, event_tx,
      crew_id, parent_sc);
}

# `agnosai_execute_task_in_crew`, with everything the task costs in allocator `a`.
fn agnosai_execute_task_in_crew_a(a, task, agent, client, event_tx, crew_id: Str,
  parent_sc): i64 {
    return agnosai_execute_task_with_tools_a(a, task, agent, client, event_tx, crew_id, parent_sc,
      0);
}

# `agnosai_execute_task_in_crew_a`, with the crew's tools (ADR 029).
#
# `loop` is from `agnosai_tool_loop_new_a`, or 0 for none: an agent's tools are then only words in
# its system prompt, which is all they were before ADR 029. The agent is offered the tools it
# names, the model calls them until it answers, and each call leaves a record on the result.
fn agnosai_execute_task_with_tools_a(a, task, agent, client, event_tx, crew_id: Str, parent_sc,
  loop): i64 {
    return agnosai_execute_task_budgeted_a(a, task, agent, client, event_tx, crew_id, parent_sc, loop,
      0);
}

# `agnosai_execute_task_with_tools_a`, held to `budget`, a tracker or 0 (ADR 033).
#
# Every model call is checked against it first, and refused once it or any tracker above it is
# spent, which fails the task with the breach as its error. What each call costs is recorded in
# it. A task with limits of its own gets a tracker of them under `budget`, so it stops at the
# tighter of its own and what its crew has left.
fn agnosai_execute_task_budgeted_a(a, task, agent, client, event_tx, crew_id: Str, parent_sc,
  loop, budget): i64 {
    # ⚠ Built ONLY with the exporter on. Off — the common case — this costs one
    # load and a compare, no clock read, and the body is handed span 0 and
    # context 0.
    var span = 0;
    var self_sc = 0;
    var span_t0 = 0;
    if (agnosai_telemetry_exporter() != 0) {
        var agent_name = 0;
        var agent_key = 0;
        if (agent != 0) {
            agent_name = agnosai_agent_name(agent);
            agent_key = agnosai_agent_key(agent);
        }
        span = agnosai_genai_agent_span(agent_name, agent_key, agnosai_task_id(task));
        self_sc = agnosai_telemetry_span_ctx(parent_sc);
        span_t0 = clock_epoch_ns();
    }

    var result = _agnosai_execute_task_body_a(a, task, agent, client, event_tx, crew_id, span,
      self_sc, loop, budget);

    # Recorded ONCE, from the result: a failed inference is a Failed result, not
    # an early return, so there is no other exit to cover.
    if (span != 0) {
        var ok = 0;
        if (agnosai_task_result_status(result) == AGNOSAI_STATUS_COMPLETED) { ok = 1; }
        else { agnosai_genai_span_set_error_type(span, str_from("task_failed")); }
        agnosai_telemetry_record_span_in(span, self_sc, parent_sc, span_t0, clock_epoch_ns(),
          agnosai_telemetry_status_of(ok));
    }
    return result;
}

# Execute one task outside a crew — the oracle's `execute_task` signature.
#
# A `token` event takes its crew id from the task's context, else `"unknown"`,
# exactly as the oracle's did. A runner calls `agnosai_execute_task_in_crew`
# instead, so its events name the crew they belong to.
fn agnosai_execute_task(task, agent, client, event_tx): i64 {
    return agnosai_execute_task_in_crew(task, agent, client, event_tx, 0, 0);
}

# ── bite 16: the CrewRunner ────────────────────

# A task queued for a worker thread: the shared inputs plus one task and its
# chosen agent, with a slot for the result.
#
# `EVENT_TX` is the crew's sender when someone is subscribed, else 0, and
# `CREW_ID` the spec's id — together they let a worker emit the `token` event
# the sequential path does (ADR 022). `TRACE` is the crew's span context, or 0:
# the job is how a worker thread gets its parent, because a `thread_create`
# worker starts with an empty thread-local block (ADR 023).
#
# `ALLOC` is the crew's allocator (ADR 028). `DONE` turns 1 when the worker is over.
# `SLOTS` and `TAIL` are its wave's: a worker takes the next slot for each thing it has to say,
# its job there as the value, and the wave waits on the slots in turn, so it hears of
# each job as it finishes, in completion order (ADR 032), and of a slot a job gave back while
# its answer waits on a reviewer (ADR 033). See `src/done_word.cyr`.
enum AgnosaiCrewJobOff {
    AGN_CJ_TASK = 0;
    AGN_CJ_AGENT = 8;
    AGN_CJ_CLIENT = 16;
    AGN_CJ_RESULT = 24;
    AGN_CJ_CANCELLED = 32;
    AGN_CJ_EVENT_TX = 40;
    AGN_CJ_CREW_ID = 48;
    AGN_CJ_TRACE = 56;
    AGN_CJ_ALLOC = 64;
    AGN_CJ_DONE = 72;
    # The crew's tools, or 0 (ADR 029).
    AGN_CJ_LOOP = 80;
    AGN_CJ_SLOTS = 88;
    AGN_CJ_TAIL = 96;
    # The runner, for a worker whose task's answer needs a reviewer (ADR 032), or 0.
    AGN_CJ_RUNNER = 104;
    # 1 once the wave has heard this job give its slot back. The wave's alone to touch.
    AGN_CJ_FREED = 112;
    AGN_CJ_SIZE = 120;
}

# What a slot in a wave says of its job, beside the job: that it gave its slot back, where the job
# alone says it is over. A job is 8-aligned, so the two cannot be mistaken.
var _AGNOSAI_CREW_JOB_FREED = 1;

# Give a job's slot back to its wave while its answer waits on a reviewer (ADR 033), so the wave
# can start another task. The job is not over: it still says so, later. Nothing outside a wave.
fn _agnosai_crew_job_free_slot(job): i64 {
    var slots = load64(job + AGN_CJ_SLOTS);
    if (slots == 0) { return 0; }
    var k = atomic_fetch_add(load64(job + AGN_CJ_TAIL), 1);
    agnosai_done_word_set_value(slots + k * 8, job + _AGNOSAI_CREW_JOB_FREED);
    return 0;
}

# Say a job is over: `DONE`, then its wave's next slot, which is the worker's last touch of
# anything the wave holds. A job outside a wave (`SLOTS` 0) has only `DONE`.
fn _agnosai_crew_job_done(job): i64 {
    var slots = load64(job + AGN_CJ_SLOTS);
    var tail = load64(job + AGN_CJ_TAIL);
    agnosai_done_word_set(job + AGN_CJ_DONE);
    if (slots == 0) { return 0; }
    var k = atomic_fetch_add(tail, 1);
    agnosai_done_word_set_value(slots + k * 8, job);
    return 0;
}

# Say a job is over, and wake the wave if it is parked on that.
#
# One worker thread — a thread passes exactly one argument.
#
# Cancellation is checked here rather than only before spawning, matching the
# oracle's second check after acquiring its semaphore permit: a crew cancelled
# while a wave is in flight must not start work that was merely queued. A job
# cancelled here records no `invoke_agent` span — no agent was invoked.
fn _agnosai_crew_worker(job): i64 {
    var task = load64(job + AGN_CJ_TASK);
    var a = load64(job + AGN_CJ_ALLOC);
    var cancelled = load64(job + AGN_CJ_CANCELLED);
    if (cancelled != 0 && load64(cancelled) != 0) {
        store64(job + AGN_CJ_RESULT, agnosai_task_result_new_a(a, agnosai_task_id(task),
            str_from_a(a, "crew cancelled"), AGNOSAI_STATUS_FAILED));
        _agnosai_crew_job_done(job);
        return 0;
    }
    var runner = load64(job + AGN_CJ_RUNNER);
    var result = agnosai_execute_task_budgeted_a(a, task,
      load64(job + AGN_CJ_AGENT), load64(job + AGN_CJ_CLIENT), load64(job + AGN_CJ_EVENT_TX),
      load64(job + AGN_CJ_CREW_ID), load64(job + AGN_CJ_TRACE), load64(job + AGN_CJ_LOOP),
      agnosai_crew_runner_budget(runner));
    # On this worker's thread, so a task waiting on its reviewer holds up no other. ⚠ **And it
    # gives its slot back first (ADR 033)**: a slot is for work, and waiting is not, so a crew's
    # other tasks go on while a reviewer takes minutes. Through 2.4.0 it held its slot.
    if (runner != 0) {
        if (_agnosai_crew_needs_review(runner, task, result) == 1) { _agnosai_crew_job_free_slot(job); }
        result = _agnosai_crew_review_a(a, runner, task, load64(job + AGN_CJ_AGENT), result,
          load64(job + AGN_CJ_EVENT_TX));
    }
    store64(job + AGN_CJ_RESULT, result);
    _agnosai_crew_job_done(job);
    return 0;
}

# The runner: a crew spec plus the collaborators the oracle's builders attach.
enum AgnosaiCrewRunnerOff {
    AGN_CR_SPEC = 0;
    AGN_CR_CLIENT = 8;
    AGN_CR_EVENT_TX = 16;
    AGN_CR_CANCELLED = 24;
    AGN_CR_AUDIT = 32;
    AGN_CR_SCRATCH = 40;
    # Monotonic nanosecond deadline, or 0 for none. Set by the orchestrator from
    # the resource budget — see `agnosai_crew_runner_set_deadline_ns`.
    AGN_CR_DEADLINE_NS = 48;
    # Raised to 1 when a loop head observes the deadline has passed, so the
    # orchestrator can tell a timeout from an ordinary cancellation.
    AGN_CR_TIMED_OUT = 56;
    # The crew's `invoke_workflow` span context while it runs, or 0 — every
    # task's parent (ADR 023). Set by `agnosai_crew_runner_run`.
    AGN_CR_TRACE = 64;
    # The crew's allocator (ADR 028), read once from the spec.
    AGN_CR_ALLOC = 72;
    # The registry its agents' tools come from, or 0, and the journal, or 0 (ADR 029).
    AGN_CR_TOOLS = 80;
    AGN_CR_JOURNAL = 88;
    # What every task is handed, built when the run starts: a deadline may be set up to then.
    AGN_CR_LOOP = 96;
    # The gate a task whose answer needs a reviewer waits on, or 0, and whether `medium` risk
    # needs one too (ADR 032). With no gate such a task is refused.
    AGN_CR_GATE = 104;
    AGN_CR_APPROVE_MEDIUM = 112;
    # The crew's registered state, whose status the approval roll-up moves, or 0 for a runner
    # outside an orchestrator. And how many of its tasks await a decision now.
    AGN_CR_STATE = 120;
    AGN_CR_AWAITING = 128;
    # The crew's budget, a tracker whose parent is its tenant's, or 0 for none (ADR 033). And 1 once
    # a loop head found it spent and stopped dispatching, as `TIMED_OUT` is for the deadline.
    AGN_CR_BUDGET = 136;
    AGN_CR_BUDGET_OUT = 144;
    AGN_CR_SIZE = 152;
}

# Initial chunk for the sequential run's audit scratch.
#
# **1 KiB, chosen by measurement rather than by feel.** The chunk comes from the
# no-free global bump, so its capacity is a permanent per-run cost — too large
# and it eats the saving it was meant to produce. Measured on a 4-task run:
# 512 B -> 26,344 · **1,024 B -> 26,296** · 2,048 B -> 27,304 · 4,096 B -> 27,288.
# Below 1 KiB the arena grows an extra time and pays for a second chunk; above
# it, the slack is dead weight.
var AGNOSAI_CREW_SCRATCH_BYTES = 1024;

# Create a runner over a crew spec. Everything optional starts absent.
fn agnosai_crew_runner_new(spec): i64 {
    var a = agnosai_crew_alloc(spec);
    var r = alloc_via(a, AGN_CR_SIZE);
    store64(r + AGN_CR_SCRATCH, 0);
    store64(r + AGN_CR_SPEC, spec);
    store64(r + AGN_CR_CLIENT, 0);
    store64(r + AGN_CR_EVENT_TX, 0);
    store64(r + AGN_CR_CANCELLED, alloc_via(a, 8));
    store64(load64(r + AGN_CR_CANCELLED), 0);
    store64(r + AGN_CR_ALLOC, a);
    store64(r + AGN_CR_AUDIT, 0);
    store64(r + AGN_CR_DEADLINE_NS, 0);
    store64(r + AGN_CR_TIMED_OUT, 0);
    store64(r + AGN_CR_TRACE, 0);
    store64(r + AGN_CR_TOOLS, 0);
    store64(r + AGN_CR_JOURNAL, 0);
    store64(r + AGN_CR_LOOP, 0);
    store64(r + AGN_CR_GATE, 0);
    store64(r + AGN_CR_APPROVE_MEDIUM, 0);
    store64(r + AGN_CR_STATE, 0);
    store64(r + AGN_CR_AWAITING, 0);
    store64(r + AGN_CR_BUDGET, 0);
    store64(r + AGN_CR_BUDGET_OUT, 0);
    return r;
}

# Hold the crew to `tracker` (ADR 033), from `agnosai_budget_tracker_limits_a`, or 0 for none. Every
# model call of its tasks is checked against it and recorded in it, and the crew stops once it, or
# a tracker above it, is spent. The orchestrator gives each crew one. Returns the runner.
fn agnosai_crew_runner_with_budget(r, tracker): i64 {
    store64(r + AGN_CR_BUDGET, tracker);
    return r;
}

# The crew's budget tracker, or 0. A runner of 0 has none.
fn agnosai_crew_runner_budget(r): i64 {
    if (r == 0) { return 0; }
    return load64(r + AGN_CR_BUDGET);
}

# Whether a loop head found the crew's budget spent, and stopped dispatching.
fn agnosai_crew_runner_budget_exceeded(r): i64 {
    return load64(r + AGN_CR_BUDGET_OUT);
}

# Whether the crew's budget, or one above it, is spent: checked at each loop head, as the deadline
# is, so a crew with nothing left starts no more tasks. Raises `BUDGET_OUT` when it is.
fn _agnosai_crew_budget_spent(r): i64 {
    var t = load64(r + AGN_CR_BUDGET);
    if (t == 0) { return 0; }
    if (agnosai_budget_tracker_breach(t, 0) == AGNOSAI_BUDGET_WITHIN) { return 0; }
    store64(r + AGN_CR_BUDGET_OUT, 1);
    return 1;
}

# Give the runner the gate a task whose answer needs a reviewer waits on, and say whether a
# `medium` task needs one as well as a `high` one (`require_medium` 1). Returns the runner.
#
# ⚠ **With no gate, a task that needs a reviewer is refused** (ADR 032): its answer is
# withheld and it fails, as there is nobody to approve it. The orchestrator gives every runner
# its own gate (`agnosai_orchestrator_approval_gate`).
fn agnosai_crew_runner_with_approval(r, gate, require_medium): i64 {
    store64(r + AGN_CR_GATE, gate);
    var m = 0;
    if (require_medium == 1) { m = 1; }
    store64(r + AGN_CR_APPROVE_MEDIUM, m);
    return r;
}

# The runner's approval gate, or 0.
fn agnosai_crew_runner_approval_gate(r): i64 {
    return load64(r + AGN_CR_GATE);
}

# How many of the crew's tasks are waiting on a reviewer now.
fn agnosai_crew_runner_awaiting(r): i64 {
    return atomic_load(r + AGN_CR_AWAITING);
}

# The registered state whose status the approval roll-up moves (the orchestrator's).
fn _agnosai_crew_runner_with_state(r, state): i64 {
    store64(r + AGN_CR_STATE, state);
    return r;
}

# Attach the LLM client. Without one, every task takes the placeholder path.
fn agnosai_crew_runner_with_llm(r, client): i64 {
    store64(r + AGN_CR_CLIENT, client);
    return r;
}

# Attach an event sender for `crew_started` / `task_*` / `crew_completed`.
fn agnosai_crew_runner_with_events(r, event_tx): i64 {
    store64(r + AGN_CR_EVENT_TX, event_tx);
    return r;
}

# Attach an audit chain.
fn agnosai_crew_runner_with_audit(r, chain): i64 {
    store64(r + AGN_CR_AUDIT, chain);
    return r;
}

# Attach the registry this crew's agents get their tools from, and a journal (either may be 0).
# Without a registry an agent's tools are words in its prompt (ADR 029).
fn agnosai_crew_runner_with_tools(r, registry, journal): i64 {
    store64(r + AGN_CR_TOOLS, registry);
    store64(r + AGN_CR_JOURNAL, journal);
    return r;
}

# The shared cancellation flag — a pointer to one i64, so a worker sees a
# cancellation raised after it was spawned.
fn agnosai_crew_runner_cancel_flag(r): i64 {
    return load64(r + AGN_CR_CANCELLED);
}

# Set the execution deadline as a monotonic nanosecond timestamp, or 0 for none.
#
# ⚠ **This is a COOPERATIVE deadline, checked at the loop head of all THREE
# process modes** — sequential (`:991`), parallel/wave (`:1076`) and DAG
# (`:1155`) — not the oracle's `tokio::time::timeout`, which drops the whole
# `runner.run()` future and so aborts work in flight
# (`rust-old/src/orchestrator/orchestrator.rs:204`). The observable contract
# matches for a multi-task crew: no task starts after the deadline, and the
# runner itself returns the oracle's timeout state — `Failed`, no results, no
# profile — and says `failed` in its `crew_completed` (ADR 022). The
# orchestrator's own substitution (`_agnosai_orch_timeout_state`) still runs,
# for its warning, and is idempotent: it replaces that state with an equal one.
#
# ⚠ **Parallel was missed until 2026-08-13.** The comment here used to say "the
# two loop heads that already check cancellation", and the wave loop checked
# cancellation per job inside `_agnosai_crew_worker` rather than at its head — so
# the two were never co-located and Parallel observed no deadline at all.
# `agnosai_crew_runner_timed_out` stayed 0 and a crew over its
# `budget.max_duration_secs` reported success.
#
# ⚠ **What does NOT match: a single task that hangs forever.** Cyrius has no way
# to abort a thread mid-syscall, so the check cannot fire until the current task
# returns. `src/llm/hoosh.cyr` sets no per-request timeout, so a wedged gateway
# connection is exactly that case and still holds the worker. Closing it needs a
# request timeout on the LLM transport, which is its own bite.
fn agnosai_crew_runner_set_deadline_ns(r, ns): i64 {
    store64(r + AGN_CR_DEADLINE_NS, ns);
    return r;
}

# Whether a loop head observed the deadline pass. Distinct from cancellation:
# a timeout is reported differently, and only a timeout discards results —
# `agnosai_crew_runner_run` does both itself (ADR 022).
fn agnosai_crew_runner_timed_out(r): i64 {
    if (load64(r + AGN_CR_TIMED_OUT) != 0) { return 1; }
    return 0;
}

# True when a deadline is set and has passed. Raises the timed-out flag as a
# side effect so the orchestrator can distinguish it from a cancel.
#
# ⚠ `>=` rather than `>` is the correct comparison but is **NOT observable**, and
# an earlier version of this comment claimed otherwise. Mutation-verified
# 2026-08-12: flipping it to `>` leaves `tests/orch_orchestrator.tcyr` fully
# green. The case it would matter for — a deadline landing on exactly the
# current nanosecond — needs `clock_now_ns()` to hit one specific value, and the
# zero-second budget that motivated the claim is unreachable anyway, because
# `agnosai_orchestrator_timeout_secs` maps any `secs <= 0` to the 3600 s default.
fn _agnosai_crew_deadline_passed(r): i64 {
    var dl = load64(r + AGN_CR_DEADLINE_NS);
    if (dl == 0) { return 0; }
    if (clock_now_ns() >= dl) {
        store64(r + AGN_CR_TIMED_OUT, 1);
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

# Request cancellation. The runner stops scheduling new work; tasks already
# running finish.
fn agnosai_crew_runner_cancel(r): i64 {
    store64(load64(r + AGN_CR_CANCELLED), 1);
    return 0;
}

# Whether cancellation has been requested.
fn agnosai_crew_runner_is_cancelled(r): i64 {
    if (load64(load64(r + AGN_CR_CANCELLED)) != 0) { return 1; }
    return 0;
}

# Emit a crew event, when a sender is attached.
# Whether an event payload is worth building at all.
#
# ## Why this is a guard on *construction*, not just on sending
#
# `_agnosai_crew_emit` already returns early when no bus is attached — but the
# caller has built the payload by then, and `agnosai_event_sender_send` with
# zero subscribers takes the mutex, iterates nothing and drops it. Measured,
# the two per-task payloads cost **344 + 304 = 648 bytes**, roughly 28% of what
# `orch_crew_runner` allocates on its own for a four-task crew — all of it
# thrown away.
#
# **Zero subscribers is the normal state.** A client only attaches to
# `/api/v1/crews/{id}/stream` when someone is watching a specific crew, so a
# server with a bus configured and nobody looking is the common case, not an
# edge one.
#
# ## Why skipping is not a lost event
#
# With zero subscribers `send` has nothing to deliver to — the payload is
# discarded either way, so nothing observable changes. A subscriber attaching
# between this check and the send would miss that one event, which is already
# the contract: subscriptions are `chan_lossy`, a slow client is told it
# `Lagged` rather than served a backlog, and a client attaching mid-crew never
# receives the events that preceded it.
#
# The oracle builds its `serde_json::json!` payload before checking `event_tx`
# too. This is a divergence in *when the work happens*, not in what any client
# can observe.
fn _agnosai_crew_wants_events(r): i64 {
    var tx = load64(r + AGN_CR_EVENT_TX);
    if (tx == 0) { return 0; }
    if (agnosai_event_sender_receiver_count(tx) == 0) { return 0; }
    return 1;
}

fn _agnosai_crew_emit(r, event_type: Str, data): i64 {
    var tx = load64(r + AGN_CR_EVENT_TX);
    if (tx == 0) { return 0; }
    return agnosai_event_sender_send(tx, agnosai_crew_event_new_a(load64(r + AGN_CR_ALLOC),
        agnosai_crew_id(load64(r + AGN_CR_SPEC)), event_type, data));
}

# Record an audit event, when a chain is attached.
# ## The audit scratch, and why it is only set on one path
#
# `agnosai_audit_record_a` takes an allocator for the JSON tree it builds and
# throws away per entry — measured at ~4.6 KB per task, the largest single
# consumer in a crew run. `AGN_CR_SCRATCH` carries a growable arena for that.
#
# **It is set only while running sequentially, and is 0 otherwise.** The runner
# is shared by every worker in `_agnosai_crew_run_parallel` and
# `_agnosai_crew_run_dag`, so a scratch read off `r` would be reset by one
# thread while another was mid-hash — silent corruption of a tamper-evident log.
# 0 means "use the global allocator", which is what those paths get, and is
# exactly the behaviour they had before.
fn _agnosai_crew_audit(r, event: Str, level: Str, message: Str, metadata): i64 {
    var chain = load64(r + AGN_CR_AUDIT);
    if (chain == 0) { return 0; }
    var scratch = load64(r + AGN_CR_SCRATCH);
    if (scratch == 0) { scratch = default_alloc(); }
    var e = agnosai_audit_record_a(scratch, chain, event, level, message, 0, 0, metadata);
    if (load64(r + AGN_CR_SCRATCH) != 0) { reset_via(scratch); }
    return e;
}

# The `task_started` payload: id, description, and the agent key or null.
fn _agnosai_crew_started_data_a(a, task, agent): i64 {
    var d = bayan_json_v_obj_new_a(a);
    bayan_json_v_obj_set_a(a, d, str_from_a(a, "task_id"),
      bayan_json_v_str_new_a(a, agnosai_task_id(task)));
    bayan_json_v_obj_set_a(a, d, str_from_a(a, "description"),
      bayan_json_v_str_new_a(a, agnosai_task_description(task)));
    if (agent == 0) {
        bayan_json_v_obj_set_a(a, d, str_from_a(a, "agent"), bayan_json_v_null_a(a));
    } else {
        bayan_json_v_obj_set_a(a, d, str_from_a(a, "agent"),
          bayan_json_v_str_new_a(a, agnosai_agent_key(agent)));
    }
    return d;
}

# The `task_completed` payload: id and status.
fn _agnosai_crew_completed_data_a(a, result): i64 {
    var d = bayan_json_v_obj_new_a(a);
    bayan_json_v_obj_set_a(a, d, str_from_a(a, "task_id"),
      bayan_json_v_str_new_a(a, agnosai_task_result_task_id(result)));
    bayan_json_v_obj_set_a(a, d, str_from_a(a, "status"), bayan_json_v_str_new_a(a,
        agnosai_task_status_to_wire(agnosai_task_result_status(result))));
    return d;
}

# Audit one finished task — `run_sequential`'s only audit call site.
#
# The oracle audits from `run_sequential` alone: `run_parallel` and `run_dag`
# never call it. Reproduced rather than "fixed", because making the other two
# audit would change what a consumer's trail contains.
fn _agnosai_crew_audit_task(r, task, result, agent): i64 {
    if (load64(r + AGN_CR_AUDIT) == 0) { return 0; }
    var level = str_from("info");
    if (agnosai_task_result_status(result) != AGNOSAI_STATUS_COMPLETED) {
        level = str_from("error");
    }
    # ⚠ **On the global heap, and owning its strings, whatever the crew lives in.** The
    # chain keeps this for as long as it keeps the entry, so it outlives an arena crew,
    # and a JSON string value holds its Str by pointer (ADR 028).
    var md = bayan_json_v_obj_new();
    bayan_json_v_obj_set(md, str_from("crew_id"),
      bayan_json_v_str_new(str_clone(agnosai_crew_id(load64(r + AGN_CR_SPEC)))));
    bayan_json_v_obj_set(md, str_from("task_id"),
      bayan_json_v_str_new(str_clone(agnosai_task_result_task_id(result))));
    bayan_json_v_obj_set(md, str_from("status"),
      bayan_json_v_str_new(agnosai_task_status_to_wire(agnosai_task_result_status(result))));
    if (agent == 0) { bayan_json_v_obj_set(md, str_from("agent"), bayan_json_v_null()); }
    else {
        bayan_json_v_obj_set(md, str_from("agent"),
          bayan_json_v_str_new(str_clone(agnosai_agent_key(agent))));
    }
    return _agnosai_crew_audit(r, str_from("task_completed"), level,
      agnosai_task_description(task), md);
}

# ── the approval gate (ADR 032) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A task whose risk needs a reviewer — `high`, or `medium` when the runner says so — has its
# answer held after the model gives it: the task goes `awaiting_approval`, `approval_requested`
# carries the answer to whoever watches, and the task waits on the gate until a reviewer
# decides, the gate's own timeout passes, or the crew is cancelled or out of time. An approved
# answer stands. Anything else is a rejection: the task fails and its answer is withheld, so it
# reaches neither the crew's results nor a dependent task. Every way of not deciding rejects,
# as the gate always has (`orchestrator/approval.cyr`).
#
# **Only a completed answer is reviewed.** A task that failed has nothing to approve.
#
# The crew rolls up: it reads `awaiting_approval` while any of its tasks does, as herdr's
# `blocked` does. A cancel overrides it, as a terminal state is never moved again.

# One lock for every crew's roll-up: it is taken only when a task starts or stops waiting on a
# reviewer, so it is never contended in earnest, and it costs no crew an allocation of its own.
var _AGNOSAI_CREW_ROLLUP_LOCK = mutex_new();

# Move the crew's count of tasks awaiting a decision by `delta`, and its registered status
# with it: RUNNING to AWAITING_APPROVAL as the first task starts waiting, and back as the last
# stops. Each by compare-and-swap, so a cancel that landed between stands.
fn _agnosai_crew_rollup(r, delta): i64 {
    mutex_lock(_AGNOSAI_CREW_ROLLUP_LOCK);
    var n = load64(r + AGN_CR_AWAITING) + delta;
    atomic_store(r + AGN_CR_AWAITING, n);
    var st = load64(r + AGN_CR_STATE);
    if (st != 0) {
        if (delta > 0 && n == 1) {
            atomic_cas(st + AGN_CSTATE_STATUS, AGNOSAI_CREW_RUNNING, AGNOSAI_CREW_AWAITING_APPROVAL);
        }
        if (delta < 0 && n == 0) {
            atomic_cas(st + AGN_CSTATE_STATUS, AGNOSAI_CREW_AWAITING_APPROVAL, AGNOSAI_CREW_RUNNING);
        }
    }
    mutex_unlock(_AGNOSAI_CREW_ROLLUP_LOCK);
    return n;
}

# Whether `result`, the answer to `task`, needs a reviewer before it stands.
fn _agnosai_crew_needs_review(r, task, result): i64 {
    if (agnosai_task_result_status(result) != AGNOSAI_STATUS_COMPLETED) { return 0; }
    return agnosai_requires_approval(agnosai_task_risk(task), load64(r + AGN_CR_APPROVE_MEDIUM));
}

# Send `approval_requested` on `tx`, when someone is subscribed: the answer a reviewer judges.
fn _agnosai_crew_emit_review_a(a, r, tx, task, agent, result): i64 {
    if (tx == 0) { return 0; }
    if (agnosai_event_sender_receiver_count(tx) == 0) { return 0; }
    var key = 0;
    if (agent != 0) { key = agnosai_agent_key(agent); }
    var req = alloc_via(a, AGN_AR_SIZE);
    store64(req + AGN_AR_TASK_ID, agnosai_task_id(task));
    store64(req + AGN_AR_OUTPUT, agnosai_task_result_output(result));
    store64(req + AGN_AR_RISK, agnosai_task_risk(task));
    store64(req + AGN_AR_AGENT, key);
    return agnosai_event_sender_send(tx, agnosai_crew_event_new_a(a,
        agnosai_crew_id(load64(r + AGN_CR_SPEC)), str_from_a(a, "approval_requested"),
        agnosai_approval_request_to_value_a(a, req)));
}

# Send `approval_decided` on `tx`, when someone is subscribed.
fn _agnosai_crew_emit_decided_a(a, r, tx, task, decision, reason: Str): i64 {
    if (tx == 0) { return 0; }
    if (agnosai_event_sender_receiver_count(tx) == 0) { return 0; }
    var d = bayan_json_v_obj_new_a(a);
    bayan_json_v_obj_set_a(a, d, str_from_a(a, "task_id"), bayan_json_v_str_new_a(a, agnosai_task_id(task)));
    bayan_json_v_obj_set_a(a, d, str_from_a(a, "decision"),
      bayan_json_v_str_new_a(a, agnosai_approval_decision_to_wire(decision)));
    bayan_json_v_obj_set_a(a, d, str_from_a(a, "reason"), bayan_json_v_str_new_a(a, reason));
    return agnosai_event_sender_send(tx, agnosai_crew_event_new_a(a,
        agnosai_crew_id(load64(r + AGN_CR_SPEC)), str_from_a(a, "approval_decided"), d));
}

# Audit a decision, when a chain is attached: who decided what is the trail's business. On the
# global heap and owning its strings, as the entry outlives a crew in an arena (ADR 028).
fn _agnosai_crew_audit_decided(r, task, decision, reason: Str): i64 {
    if (load64(r + AGN_CR_AUDIT) == 0) { return 0; }
    var level = str_from("info");
    if (decision != AGNOSAI_APPROVAL_APPROVED) { level = str_from("warn"); }
    var md = bayan_json_v_obj_new();
    bayan_json_v_obj_set(md, str_from("crew_id"),
      bayan_json_v_str_new(str_clone(agnosai_crew_id(load64(r + AGN_CR_SPEC)))));
    bayan_json_v_obj_set(md, str_from("task_id"), bayan_json_v_str_new(str_clone(agnosai_task_id(task))));
    bayan_json_v_obj_set(md, str_from("decision"),
      bayan_json_v_str_new(agnosai_approval_decision_to_wire(decision)));
    bayan_json_v_obj_set(md, str_from("reason"), bayan_json_v_str_new(reason));
    return _agnosai_crew_audit(r, str_from("approval_decided"), level, agnosai_task_description(task), md);
}

# Hold `result` for a reviewer when `task` needs one, and return what stands: `result` as it was,
# or, rejected, the same result FAILED with its answer withheld. `tx` is the sender this task's
# events go on, or 0. In `a`, the crew's allocator.
fn _agnosai_crew_review_a(a, r, task, agent, result, tx): i64 {
    if (_agnosai_crew_needs_review(r, task, result) == 0) { return result; }
    var task_id = agnosai_task_id(task);
    agnosai_task_with_status(task, AGNOSAI_STATUS_AWAITING_APPROVAL);
    _agnosai_crew_rollup(r, 1);

    # Registered before it is announced, so a reviewer acting on the event finds it pending.
    var gate = load64(r + AGN_CR_GATE);
    var ch = 0;
    if (gate != 0) { ch = agnosai_approval_request(gate, task_id); }
    _agnosai_crew_emit_review_a(a, r, tx, task, agent, result);

    var why: i64[1];
    store64(&why, AGNOSAI_APPROVAL_REASON_NO_GATE);
    var decision = AGNOSAI_APPROVAL_REJECTED;
    if (gate != 0) {
        decision = agnosai_approval_wait_until(gate, ch, task_id, load64(r + AGN_CR_CANCELLED),
          load64(r + AGN_CR_DEADLINE_NS), &why);
    }
    var reason = load64(&why);
    # Out of time while it waited: the crew has timed out, as it would at its next loop head.
    if (reason == AGNOSAI_APPROVAL_REASON_DEADLINE) { store64(r + AGN_CR_TIMED_OUT, 1); }

    _agnosai_crew_rollup(r, 0 - 1);
    agnosai_task_with_status(task, AGNOSAI_STATUS_RUNNING);
    _agnosai_crew_emit_decided_a(a, r, tx, task, decision, reason);
    _agnosai_crew_audit_decided(r, task, decision, reason);
    agnosai_task_result_with_metadata_a(a, result, str_from_a(a, "approval"),
      bayan_json_v_str_new_a(a, agnosai_approval_decision_to_wire(decision)));
    agnosai_task_result_with_metadata_a(a, result, str_from_a(a, "approval_reason"),
      bayan_json_v_str_new_a(a, reason));
    if (decision == AGNOSAI_APPROVAL_APPROVED) { return result; }

    if (sakshi_get_level() >= SK_WARN) {
        var f[AGNOSAI_LOG_FIELDS_BYTES];
        var n = agnosai_log_fields_start(&f, task_id);
        n = agnosai_log_field_str(&f, n, "reason", reason);
        sakshi_log_kv(SK_WARN, "task output withheld: approval rejected", 39, "task_id", 7, &f, n);
    }
    var sb = str_builder_new_a(a);
    str_builder_add_cstr_a(a, sb, "output withheld: approval rejected (");
    str_builder_add_a(a, sb, reason);
    str_builder_add_cstr_a(a, sb, ")");
    agnosai_task_result_with_output(result, str_builder_build_a(a, sb));
    return agnosai_task_result_with_status(result, AGNOSAI_STATUS_FAILED);
}

# Run every task in order on this thread — the oracle's `run_sequential`.
#
# Checks cancellation before each task and stops rather than failing the rest,
# so a cancelled crew's results hold what actually ran.
fn _agnosai_crew_run_sequential(r): i64 {
    # One growable scratch for the whole run, reset after every audit entry.
    # Growable rather than fixed because an entry's metadata is caller-supplied
    # and unbounded; because it is reset each time, it converges on the
    # high-water mark and then stops allocating.
    #
    # **Only when there is a chain to write to.** An arena's backing chunk comes
    # from the no-free global bump, so creating one costs its capacity
    # permanently — measured, an unconditional scratch made an audit-less run
    # *4.2 KB worse* rather than better. The whole point is to spend that once
    # and amortise it over many entries, which cannot happen if there are none.
    if (load64(r + AGN_CR_AUDIT) != 0) {
        store64(r + AGN_CR_SCRATCH, arena_allocator_growable(AGNOSAI_CREW_SCRATCH_BYTES));
    }
    var spec = load64(r + AGN_CR_SPEC);
    var tasks = agnosai_crew_tasks(spec);
    var agents = agnosai_crew_agents(spec);
    var client = load64(r + AGN_CR_CLIENT);
    var a = load64(r + AGN_CR_ALLOC);
    var results = vec_new_a(a);
    # Each result by its task's id, for a later task that depends on it (ADR 034). A sequential
    # crew runs its tasks in the order they are listed, so a task is handed the answers of the
    # dependencies listed before it that completed, and says of any other that it has none.
    var by_id = map_new_str_a(a);

    # Two event names, minted once rather than once per task.
    var ev_started = str_from_a(a, "task_started");
    var ev_completed = str_from_a(a, "task_completed");
    for (var i = 0; i < vec_len(tasks); i = i + 1) {
        if (agnosai_crew_runner_is_cancelled(r) == 1) { break; }
        if (_agnosai_crew_deadline_passed(r) == 1) { break; }
        if (_agnosai_crew_budget_spent(r) == 1) { break; }

        # ⚠ **Asked per task, not once per run (ADR 032).** A subscriber that attached after the
        # run began got nothing until `crew_completed` through 2.3.8. The task's sender goes to it
        # only when someone is subscribed, so an unwatched bus builds no token either, as a wave's
        # workers already did not.
        var wants_events = _agnosai_crew_wants_events(r);
        var task_tx = 0;
        if (wants_events == 1) { task_tx = load64(r + AGN_CR_EVENT_TX); }
        var task = vec_get(tasks, i);
        var agent = _agnosai_crew_pick_best_agent_a(a, agents, task);
        agnosai_task_with_status(task, AGNOSAI_STATUS_QUEUED);
        if (wants_events == 1) {
            _agnosai_crew_emit(r, ev_started, _agnosai_crew_started_data_a(a, task, agent));
        }

        agnosai_task_with_status(task, AGNOSAI_STATUS_RUNNING);
        agnosai_task_with_handoff(task, agnosai_handoff_render_a(a, task, by_id));
        var result = agnosai_execute_task_budgeted_a(a, task, agent, client,
          task_tx, agnosai_crew_id(spec), load64(r + AGN_CR_TRACE),
          load64(r + AGN_CR_LOOP), load64(r + AGN_CR_BUDGET));
        result = _agnosai_crew_review_a(a, r, task, agent, result, task_tx);
        agnosai_task_with_handoff(task, 0);
        agnosai_task_with_status(task, agnosai_task_result_status(result));
        map_set_a(a, by_id, agnosai_task_id(task), result);

        if (wants_events == 1) {
            _agnosai_crew_emit(r, ev_completed, _agnosai_crew_completed_data_a(a, result));
        }
        _agnosai_crew_audit_task(r, task, result, agent);
        vec_push_a(a, results, result);
    }
    store64(r + AGN_CR_SCRATCH, 0);
    return results;
}

# Run a set of tasks concurrently, at most `max_concurrency` at a time.
#
# One OS thread per task, `max_concurrency` of them at a time, and the next started as
# one ends (ADR 033). The wave is the only dispatcher, so the limit needs no semaphore:
# it counts what it started and what it heard end. `alloc` has been thread-safe since
# cyrius 6.0.64, which is what makes real threads viable here at all.
#
# ⚠ **Detached, and waited on through the job (2.2.5).** `thread_create` allocates a
# handle and 4 KiB of TLS on the no-free bump, and `thread_join` gives back only the
# stack, so a crew cost over 4 KiB per task for good, in any arena. A detached thread
# keeps both in its own mapping and unmaps it, and allocates nothing, which is why
# `agnosai_orchestrator_submit_crew` uses one too. It leaves no handle to join, so the
# wave's slots do that work: a worker that is over puts its job in the next one.
#
# `max_concurrency` of 0 or less runs everything at once, matching the
# oracle's `Semaphore::new(0)` being a caller error rather than a stall.
#
# ## Events say what happened, when it happened (ADR 022)
#
# **`task_started` is emitted as a task is dispatched to its worker**, which is
# when a slot comes free for it, and **`task_completed` as its worker finishes**, in
# completion order (ADR 032). A task the deadline or a cancel stopped before
# dispatch gets neither. The oracle emits `task_started` for a whole parallel crew
# up front (`crew_runner.rs:337-348` as of tag 2.1.4) — the port did the same and
# also held every `task_completed` until the last batch had joined, so a watcher
# saw a whole wave "started" and could not tell running from done. The oracle
# emits `task_completed` per join (`:400-411`, DAG `:521-535`), in completion
# order: `join_next` yields whichever task finishes first. ADR 022 moved back to
# per-join reporting in dispatch order, and ADR 032 to completion order, which
# is the oracle's. The results stay in dispatch order, where the oracle's are in
# completion order, and `task_started` stays at dispatch.
#
# **Workers emit `token` events**, as the sequential path does. The oracle
# passes `None` here (`:393`, `:514`), and this comment used to add "two threads
# racing on one channel" as a reason. There is no race:
# `agnosai_event_sender_send` holds the bus mutex across its whole fan-out
# (`src/server/sse.cyr`). A worker gets the sender only when someone is
# subscribed, so a bus nobody watches costs the workers nothing — exactly
# nothing: `tests/orch_crew_runner.tcyr` requires a run with an unwatched bus to
# allocate the same bytes as one with no bus.
fn _agnosai_crew_run_wave(r, tasks, agents, results): i64 {
    # Minted once for the wave rather than once per task, as in the
    # sequential path.
    var a = load64(r + AGN_CR_ALLOC);
    var ev_started = str_from_a(a, "task_started");
    var ev_completed = str_from_a(a, "task_completed");
    var client = load64(r + AGN_CR_CLIENT);
    var spec = load64(r + AGN_CR_SPEC);
    var limit = agnosai_process_max_concurrency(agnosai_crew_process(spec));
    if (limit <= 0) { limit = vec_len(tasks); }
    if (limit <= 0) { return results; }

    var crew_id = agnosai_crew_id(spec);

    var jobs = vec_new_a(a);
    for (var i = 0; i < vec_len(tasks); i = i + 1) {
        var task = vec_get(tasks, i);
        var agent = _agnosai_crew_pick_best_agent_a(a, agents, task);
        agnosai_task_with_status(task, AGNOSAI_STATUS_QUEUED);

        var job = alloc_via(a, AGN_CJ_SIZE);
        store64(job + AGN_CJ_TASK, task);
        store64(job + AGN_CJ_AGENT, agent);
        store64(job + AGN_CJ_CLIENT, client);
        store64(job + AGN_CJ_RESULT, 0);
        store64(job + AGN_CJ_CANCELLED, load64(r + AGN_CR_CANCELLED));
        # Set at dispatch, from whether anyone is subscribed then (ADR 032).
        store64(job + AGN_CJ_EVENT_TX, 0);
        store64(job + AGN_CJ_CREW_ID, crew_id);
        store64(job + AGN_CJ_TRACE, load64(r + AGN_CR_TRACE));
        store64(job + AGN_CJ_ALLOC, a);
        store64(job + AGN_CJ_DONE, 0);
        store64(job + AGN_CJ_LOOP, load64(r + AGN_CR_LOOP));
        store64(job + AGN_CJ_SLOTS, 0);
        store64(job + AGN_CJ_TAIL, 0);
        store64(job + AGN_CJ_RUNNER, r);
        store64(job + AGN_CJ_FREED, 0);
        vec_push_a(a, jobs, job);
    }

    # ⚠ **A sliding window, not batches (ADR 033).** At most `limit` tasks run at once, and a
    # task starts as soon as one ends or gives its slot back to wait on a reviewer. Through 2.4.0
    # the wave ran fixed batches of `limit`, each waited out before the next began, so one slow
    # task left the others' slots idle until it ended, and a task awaiting a reviewer held up its
    # whole batch for as long as the reviewer took. Every task the wave starts is waited for
    # before it returns, so none outlives it.
    #
    # Every word a worker posts goes in the next of `slots`, its job as the value: the job when it
    # is over, the job plus `_AGNOSAI_CREW_JOB_FREED` when it gave its slot back first. A job says
    # each at most once, so a wave of `n` needs `2n` words, and they come in the order they were
    # said, so a job's slot comes back before it is over.
    #
    # ⚠ **The deadline and a cancel are checked at each dispatch.** The oracle's timeout wraps the
    # whole run, so it fires in every process mode; the port checks at loop heads instead, and a
    # Parallel crew once never did (it ran out its time and reported success). A task already
    # started is waited for rather than abandoned: the cooperative contract of the module header.
    # A job the deadline or a cancel stopped is never dispatched, so it has no result, no
    # `task_started` and no `task_completed`: the shape the sequential break has.
    var n = vec_len(jobs);
    var slots = alloc_via(a, 2 * n * 8);
    memset(slots, 0, 2 * n * 8);
    var tail = alloc_via(a, 8);
    store64(tail, 0);
    var next = 0;
    var in_flight = 0;
    var reported = 0;
    var read = 0;
    var stopped = 0;
    while (1 == 1) {
        while (stopped == 0 && in_flight < limit && next < n) {
            if (agnosai_crew_runner_is_cancelled(r) == 1 || _agnosai_crew_deadline_passed(r) == 1
              || _agnosai_crew_budget_spent(r) == 1) {
                stopped = 1;
                break;
            }
            # ⚠ **Asked per task, not once per wave (ADR 032)**, so a subscriber that attached
            # mid-run is told about the tasks after it. A worker gets the sender only then, and its
            # `task_completed` goes out only if its `task_started` did.
            var job = vec_get(jobs, next);
            var worker_tx = 0;
            if (_agnosai_crew_wants_events(r) == 1) {
                worker_tx = load64(r + AGN_CR_EVENT_TX);
                _agnosai_crew_emit(r, ev_started, _agnosai_crew_started_data_a(a,
                    load64(job + AGN_CJ_TASK), load64(job + AGN_CJ_AGENT)));
            }
            store64(job + AGN_CJ_EVENT_TX, worker_tx);
            store64(job + AGN_CJ_SLOTS, slots);
            store64(job + AGN_CJ_TAIL, tail);
            next = next + 1;
            in_flight = in_flight + 1;
            if (thread_create_detached(&_agnosai_crew_worker, job) == 0) {
                # The thread could not be created — run it here rather than
                # dropping the task. A missing result would silently shorten the
                # crew's output. Its `task_started` is already out, before it runs.
                _agnosai_crew_worker(job);
            }
        }
        if (reported == next) { break; }

        # ⚠ **Each task is reported as it finishes, in completion order (ADR 032)**, as the
        # oracle's `join_next` did.
        var said = agnosai_done_word_wait_value(slots + read * 8);
        read = read + 1;
        var jb = said - (said % 8);
        if (said % 8 == _AGNOSAI_CREW_JOB_FREED) {
            store64(jb + AGN_CJ_FREED, 1);
            in_flight = in_flight - 1;
        } else {
            if (load64(jb + AGN_CJ_FREED) == 0) { in_flight = in_flight - 1; }
            reported = reported + 1;
            var res = load64(jb + AGN_CJ_RESULT);
            if (res != 0) {
                agnosai_task_with_status(load64(jb + AGN_CJ_TASK), agnosai_task_result_status(res));
                if (load64(jb + AGN_CJ_EVENT_TX) != 0) {
                    _agnosai_crew_emit(r, ev_completed, _agnosai_crew_completed_data_a(a, res));
                }
            }
        }
    }
    # The results stay in dispatch order, so a crew's state does not depend on which of its
    # tasks happened to finish first.
    for (var j = 0; j < next; j = j + 1) {
        var done = load64(vec_get(jobs, j) + AGN_CJ_RESULT);
        if (done != 0) { vec_push_a(a, results, done); }
    }
    return results;
}

# The oracle's `run_parallel` — every task at once, bounded by max_concurrency.
fn _agnosai_crew_run_parallel(r): i64 {
    var spec = load64(r + AGN_CR_SPEC);
    return _agnosai_crew_run_wave(r, agnosai_crew_tasks(spec), agnosai_crew_agents(spec),
      vec_new_a(load64(r + AGN_CR_ALLOC)));
}

# Whether every dependency of `task` is in the completed set.
fn _agnosai_crew_deps_ready(task, completed): i64 {
    var deps = agnosai_task_dependencies(task);
    for (var i = 0; i < vec_len(deps); i = i + 1) {
        if (map_get(completed, vec_get(deps, i)) == 0) { return 0; }
    }
    return 1;
}

# The oracle's `run_dag`: topologically sort, then run ready waves.
#
# Returns the results vec, or 0 on a cycle or a deadlock — the caller turns 0
# into the error, because the oracle's `?` means a cyclic spec never emits
# `crew_completed` and never builds a CrewState at all.
#
# **Only SUCCESSFUL tasks enter the completed set**, so a failed task's
# dependents never become ready. That is what makes a failure cascade rather
# than letting downstream tasks run against missing output.
#
# ⚠ **A failure is sticky across waves (2.1.6).** Until 2.1.5 the flag was
# reset every wave and the stranded check ran only on the wave that failed. A
# failed branch beside a successful one — `[A fails, X, B <- A, Y <- X]` — let
# wave 2 ({Y}) succeed and skip the check, so wave 3 found nothing ready and
# took the DAG-deadlock arm: no state, `crew_completed` never sent, and the
# registry back at PENDING forever (agnostic, which accepts that crew, read it
# `running` for good). A run whose remainder a failure stranded now ends like
# any failed run: a FAILED state with the results it has. The deadlock arm is
# left to what it was written for — a dependency on a task the spec does not
# hold, which no failure explains.
fn _agnosai_crew_run_dag(r, out_err): i64 {
    store64(out_err, 0);
    var a = load64(r + AGN_CR_ALLOC);
    var spec = load64(r + AGN_CR_SPEC);
    var tasks = agnosai_crew_tasks(spec);
    var agents = agnosai_crew_agents(spec);

    var order = agnosai_topological_sort_tasks_a(a, tasks);
    if (order == 0) {
        store64(out_err, agnosai_error_cyclic_dag());
        return 0;
    }

    # Index the tasks by id so a sorted id can find its task.
    var by_id = map_new_str_a(a);
    for (var i = 0; i < vec_len(tasks); i = i + 1) {
        map_set_a(a, by_id, agnosai_task_id(vec_get(tasks, i)), vec_get(tasks, i));
    }

    var completed = map_new_str_a(a);
    var results = vec_new_a(a);
    var remaining = order;
    var any_failed = 0;

    while (vec_len(remaining) > 0) {
        if (agnosai_crew_runner_is_cancelled(r) == 1) { break; }
        if (_agnosai_crew_deadline_passed(r) == 1) { break; }
        if (_agnosai_crew_budget_spent(r) == 1) { break; }

        # Partition into the ready front and the rest, preserving order.
        var ready = vec_new_a(a);
        var not_ready = vec_new_a(a);
        for (var j = 0; j < vec_len(remaining); j = j + 1) {
            var id = vec_get(remaining, j);
            var t = map_get(by_id, id);
            if (t != 0 && _agnosai_crew_deps_ready(t, completed) == 1) {
                vec_push_a(a, ready, t);
            } else {
                vec_push_a(a, not_ready, id);
            }
        }

        if (vec_len(ready) == 0) {
            # A failure earlier in the run stranded what is left: its dependents
            # can never become ready. That is a failed run, not a scheduling error.
            if (any_failed == 1) { break; }
            # Unreachable after a successful topological sort, but a dangling
            # dependency reaches it — a task depending on an id that is not in
            # the spec is never ready and never sorted away.
            store64(out_err, agnosai_error_scheduling(
                str_from("DAG deadlock: no ready tasks but remaining exist")));
            return 0;
        }

        # Every ready task is handed what its dependencies answered (ADR 034), which `completed`
        # holds for each, as a task becomes ready only once all of them have completed.
        for (var h = 0; h < vec_len(ready); h = h + 1) {
            var ht = vec_get(ready, h);
            agnosai_task_with_handoff(ht, agnosai_handoff_render_a(a, ht, completed));
        }
        var before = vec_len(results);
        _agnosai_crew_run_wave(r, ready, agents, results);
        for (var hc = 0; hc < vec_len(ready); hc = hc + 1) {
            agnosai_task_with_handoff(vec_get(ready, hc), 0);
        }

        for (var k = before; k < vec_len(results); k = k + 1) {
            var res = vec_get(results, k);
            if (agnosai_task_result_status(res) == AGNOSAI_STATUS_COMPLETED) {
                map_set_a(a, completed, agnosai_task_result_task_id(res), res);
            }
        }
        # A ready task that did not end COMPLETED failed — whether its result
        # says so or it produced none at all, which the result loop above would
        # never see.
        for (var q = 0; q < vec_len(ready); q = q + 1) {
            if (map_get(completed, agnosai_task_id(vec_get(ready, q))) == 0) { any_failed = 1; }
        }

        remaining = not_ready;

        # Stop early when a failure has stranded everything left, rather than
        # spinning to the deadlock arm.
        if (any_failed == 1 && vec_len(remaining) > 0) {
            var any_runnable = 0;
            for (var m = 0; m < vec_len(remaining); m = m + 1) {
                var rt = map_get(by_id, vec_get(remaining, m));
                if (rt != 0 && _agnosai_crew_deps_ready(rt, completed) == 1) { any_runnable = 1; }
            }
            if (any_runnable == 0) { break; }
        }
    }
    return results;
}

# Aggregate the crew's profile from per-task metadata.
#
# `cost_micro_usd` replaces the oracle's f64 `cost_usd` — see
# `_agnosai_crew_answer_metadata`. A task with no cost key contributes nothing,
# which is why the key is omitted rather than zeroed when a gateway does not
# report one: a zero would be indistinguishable from a free local inference and
# would still be filtered out here by the oracle's own `> 0` guard.
#
# `agent_cost_usd` is aggregated from each result's `agent` key, which the LLM
# success arm writes since ADR 022 — before it, nothing did and the map was
# always empty. `agnosai_crew_profile_record_agent_cost` sums, so an agent that
# answered several tasks is charged for all of them.
#
# The sandbox strength is worked out once: it scores a fixed policy, so it is the same
# number for every crew, and each working-out allocates a policy on the global heap.
# Two crews racing the first store write the same value.
var _AGNOSAI_CREW_SANDBOX_STRENGTH = 0 - 1;

fn _agnosai_crew_build_profile(r, results, wall_ms): i64 {
    var a = load64(r + AGN_CR_ALLOC);
    var profile = agnosai_crew_profile_new_a(a);
    agnosai_crew_profile_with_wall_ms(profile, wall_ms);
    agnosai_crew_profile_with_task_count(profile, vec_len(results));

    # ⚠ **`sandbox_strength`, which the port computed nowhere.** The oracle sets
    # it on every crew profile (`crew_runner.rs:198-214`): `SandboxPolicy::process()`
    # scored by `kavach_bridge::strength_for_policy(..).value()`. The field, its
    # setter and its serialization were all ported and covered by
    # `tests/core_crew.tcyr` — but nothing outside a test ever called the setter,
    # so every profile this server emitted **omitted** the field where the oracle
    # emits a number.
    #
    # ⚠ It sits behind `#[cfg(all(feature = "kavach", feature = "sandbox"))]` in
    # the oracle. A cargo feature gate is not a scope boundary here, so the
    # port computes it unconditionally — the `None` arm has no equivalent,
    # because both producers are always linked.
    if (_AGNOSAI_CREW_SANDBOX_STRENGTH < 0) {
        _AGNOSAI_CREW_SANDBOX_STRENGTH = agnosai_kavach_strength_for_policy(
          agnosai_sandbox_policy_process());
    }
    agnosai_crew_profile_with_sandbox_strength(profile, _AGNOSAI_CREW_SANDBOX_STRENGTH);

    var total = 0;
    # **Hoisted: three metadata keys, once instead of once per result.** Each
    # `str_from` mints a 16-byte Str on the no-free global bump, so in a loop
    # over a crew's results they are a permanent leak proportional to task
    # count — for a constant that never changes.
    var k_ms = str_from_a(a, "task_duration_ms");
    var k_cost = str_from_a(a, "cost_micro_usd");
    var k_agent = str_from_a(a, "agent");
    for (var i = 0; i < vec_len(results); i = i + 1) {
        var res = vec_get(results, i);
        var md = agnosai_task_result_metadata(res);
        var task_id = agnosai_task_result_task_id(res);

        var ms = map_get(md, k_ms);
        if (ms != 0 && bayan_json_v_is_int(ms) == 1) {
            agnosai_crew_profile_record_task_ms_a(a, profile, task_id, bayan_json_v_int(ms));
        }

        var cost = map_get(md, k_cost);
        if (cost != 0 && bayan_json_v_is_int(cost) == 1 && bayan_json_v_int(cost) > 0) {
            var c = bayan_json_v_int(cost);
            total = total + c;
            agnosai_crew_profile_record_task_cost_a(a, profile, task_id, c);
            var agent = map_get(md, k_agent);
            if (agent != 0 && bayan_json_v_is_str(agent) == 1) {
                agnosai_crew_profile_record_agent_cost_a(a, profile, bayan_json_v_str(agent), c);
            }
        }
    }
    agnosai_crew_profile_with_cost(profile, total);
    return profile;
}

# Record one crew's worth of Prometheus counters from its finished results.
#
# **This is what makes `/metrics` render anything but zeros.** [ADR 011] gave the
# endpoint agnosai's own registry, but nothing ever recorded into it — the
# producer was staged behind the M6 routes decision and the staging outlived its
# reason. The oracle records inline at `crew_runner.rs:810`/`:864`, inside the
# per-task match arms.
#
# This records in **one pass after the workers join**, rather than at the four
# result-construction sites, for three reasons: every path lands in `results`
# (success, tool error, LLM error, and the cancellation stub at `AGN_CJ_RESULT`),
# so one loop cannot miss an arm the way four call sites can drift; the token and
# cost figures are already on each result's metadata, so nothing has to be
# threaded down; and it keeps the recording off the worker threads entirely.
#
# The recorders are atomic regardless (`atomic_fetch_add`, and a CAS loop for the
# saturating active gauge — see `src/server/prometheus.cyr`), so worker-thread
# recording would have been *safe*; it is simply not needed.
#
# `tokens` is the nested `{prompt, completion, total}` object a result the model
# answered carries. A task that failed before any answer has no `tokens` key and
# contributes nothing, matching the `cost_micro_usd` convention immediately below
# it. One whose answers never met its schema has both, as those calls were paid for.
#
# The two keys are minted once for the process: this has no allocator of its own, and
# keeps nothing.
var _AGN_CREW_K_TOKENS = str_from("tokens");
var _AGN_CREW_K_COST = str_from("cost_micro_usd");

fn _agnosai_crew_record_metrics(results): i64 {
    var m = agnosai_metrics_global();
    for (var i = 0; i < vec_len(results); i = i + 1) {
        var res = vec_get(results, i);
        if (agnosai_task_result_status(res) == AGNOSAI_STATUS_COMPLETED) {
            agnosai_metrics_record_task_completed(m);
        } else {
            agnosai_metrics_record_task_failed(m);
        }

        var md = agnosai_task_result_metadata(res);
        var total_tokens = 0;
        var tok = map_get(md, _AGN_CREW_K_TOKENS);
        if (tok != 0 && bayan_json_v_is_obj(tok) == 1) {
            var t = bayan_json_v_obj_get_by_cstr(tok, "total");
            if (t != 0 && bayan_json_v_is_int(t) == 1) { total_tokens = bayan_json_v_int(t); }
        }
        var cost_micro = 0;
        var c = map_get(md, _AGN_CREW_K_COST);
        if (c != 0 && bayan_json_v_is_int(c) == 1 && bayan_json_v_int(c) > 0) {
            cost_micro = bayan_json_v_int(c);
        }
        # One inference per answered task. Skipped entirely when the task
        # recorded neither — a task with neither never reached a provider, and
        # adding a zero would inflate the inference count without moving either
        # total.
        if (total_tokens > 0 || cost_micro > 0) {
            agnosai_metrics_record_inference(m, total_tokens, cost_micro);
        }
    }
    return 0;
}

# Close the crew's `invoke_workflow` span: OK when `err_type` (a cstring) is 0,
# else ERROR carrying it as `error.type`. Nothing at all with telemetry off.
fn _agnosai_crew_span_done(span, self_sc, parent_sc, t0, err_type): i64 {
    if (span == 0) { return 0; }
    var status = AGNOSAI_OTLP_STATUS_OK;
    if (err_type != 0) {
        agnosai_genai_span_set_error_type(span, str_from(err_type));
        status = AGNOSAI_OTLP_STATUS_ERROR;
    }
    return agnosai_telemetry_record_span_in(span, self_sc, parent_sc, t0, clock_epoch_ns(),
      status);
}

# Send `crew_completed`: the crew's terminal `status`, why when it did not complete (`reason`, a
# `AGNOSAI_CREW_REASON_*` word, or 0 to leave it out), its result count and its wall time. The
# crew's last event on every exit (ADR 032).
fn _agnosai_crew_emit_completed(r, status, reason, task_count, wall_ms): i64 {
    if (_agnosai_crew_wants_events(r) == 0) { return 0; }
    var a = load64(r + AGN_CR_ALLOC);
    var done = bayan_json_v_obj_new_a(a);
    bayan_json_v_obj_set_a(a, done, str_from_a(a, "status"),
      bayan_json_v_str_new_a(a, agnosai_crew_status_to_wire(status)));
    if (reason != 0) {
        bayan_json_v_obj_set_a(a, done, str_from_a(a, "reason"), bayan_json_v_str_new_a(a, reason));
    }
    bayan_json_v_obj_set_a(a, done, str_from_a(a, "task_count"), bayan_json_v_int_new_a(a, task_count));
    bayan_json_v_obj_set_a(a, done, str_from_a(a, "wall_ms"), bayan_json_v_int_new_a(a, wall_ms));
    return _agnosai_crew_emit(r, str_from_a(a, "crew_completed"), done);
}

# Run the crew end to end — the oracle's `run`.
#
# Returns a CrewState, or 0 with an error stored through `out_err`. The split
# matters: a cyclic DAG is an ERROR, not a `Failed` crew, so it never emits
# `crew_completed` and never builds a state — the oracle's `?` at the top of
# `run` guarantees that and a consumer can tell "the spec was wrong" from "the
# work failed".
#
# **Hierarchical falls back to sequential**, with a warning, exactly as the
# oracle does. `orch_hierarchical`'s delegation exists and is tested, but wiring
# it in here would change behaviour the oracle never implemented; that was the
# oracle's own Phase 2. The oracle is frozen, so wiring it is roadmap F2 and its ADR.
fn agnosai_crew_runner_run(r, out_err): i64 {
    store64(out_err, 0);
    var start_ns = clock_now_ns();
    var a = load64(r + AGN_CR_ALLOC);
    var spec = load64(r + AGN_CR_SPEC);
    var tasks = agnosai_crew_tasks(spec);

    # ADR 017 — the GenAI crew span, `invoke_workflow {crew name}` since ADR
    # 023. It follows the SAME discipline as the `crews_active` gauge below:
    # **every exit must close it**, including the DAG-cycle error return, or a
    # crew that fails to start is recorded as a span that never ended.
    # `clock_epoch_ns` rather than the monotonic `start_ns` above, because OTLP
    # timestamps are wall clock.
    #
    # ADR 023: built only with the exporter on. Its parent is the spec's inbound
    # `traceparent` when one parses under the W3C rules — a malformed one starts
    # a new root, never an error — and its context goes on the runner, where
    # every task (and through the wave job, every worker) finds its parent.
    var parent_sc = 0;
    var span = 0;
    var self_sc = 0;
    var span_t0 = 0;
    #
    # A crew a tool started has no span of its own, with a parent to hang its tasks from: the
    # conventions say a tool delegating to a sub-agent SHOULD NOT report one, so each
    # `invoke_agent` span goes straight under the tool's (ADR 029).
    if (agnosai_telemetry_exporter() != 0) {
        parent_sc = agnosai_otlp_span_context_parse(agnosai_crew_trace_parent(spec));
        if (parent_sc != 0 && agnosai_crew_delegated(spec) == 1) { self_sc = parent_sc; }
        else {
            span = agnosai_genai_workflow_span(agnosai_crew_id(spec), agnosai_crew_name(spec),
              vec_len(tasks));
            self_sc = agnosai_telemetry_span_ctx(parent_sc);
            span_t0 = clock_epoch_ns();
        }
    }
    store64(r + AGN_CR_TRACE, self_sc);

    if (load64(r + AGN_CR_TOOLS) != 0) {
        var loop = agnosai_tool_loop_new_a(a, load64(r + AGN_CR_TOOLS),
          load64(r + AGN_CR_JOURNAL), load64(r + AGN_CR_AUDIT), load64(r + AGN_CR_CANCELLED),
          load64(r + AGN_CR_DEADLINE_NS), r + AGN_CR_TIMED_OUT, agnosai_crew_id(spec));
        # The crew's own limits, which only tighten the process's (ADR 033).
        var tl = agnosai_crew_tool_limits(spec);
        if (tl != 0) {
            agnosai_tool_loop_tighten(loop, load64(tl), load64(tl + 8), load64(tl + 16), load64(tl + 24));
        }
        store64(r + AGN_CR_LOOP, loop);
    }

    if (_agnosai_crew_wants_events(r) == 1) {
        var started = bayan_json_v_obj_new_a(a);
        bayan_json_v_obj_set_a(a, started, str_from_a(a, "name"),
          bayan_json_v_str_new_a(a, agnosai_crew_name(spec)));
        bayan_json_v_obj_set_a(a, started, str_from_a(a, "task_count"),
          bayan_json_v_int_new_a(a, vec_len(tasks)));
        _agnosai_crew_emit(r, str_from_a(a, "crew_started"), started);
    }
    # Bumps crews_total and the crews_active gauge. **Every exit below must
    # balance the gauge**, including the DAG-cycle error return — that path does
    # not emit a `crew_completed` *event* (a cyclic spec is an error, not a crew
    # that failed) but it must still decrement, or the gauge ratchets upward for
    # the life of the process on a malformed spec. Event stream and gauge are
    # different contracts; only the event one is suppressed.
    agnosai_metrics_record_crew_started(agnosai_metrics_global());

    var kind = agnosai_process_kind(agnosai_crew_process(spec));
    var results = 0;
    if (kind == AGNOSAI_PROCESS_PARALLEL) { results = _agnosai_crew_run_parallel(r); }
    elif (kind == AGNOSAI_PROCESS_DAG) {
        var dag_err: i64[1];
        store64(&dag_err, 0);
        results = _agnosai_crew_run_dag(r, &dag_err);
        if (load64(&dag_err) != 0) {
            # Balance the active gauge before the error return. No task ran, so
            # no task/inference counters are recorded — only the gauge unwinds.
            agnosai_metrics_record_crew_completed(agnosai_metrics_global());
            _agnosai_crew_span_done(span, self_sc, parent_sc, span_t0, "invalid_dag");
            # ⚠ **A last word, so a subscriber is not left waiting (ADR 032).** Through 2.3.8
            # this arm sent nothing, and the orchestrator put the crew back to PENDING, so a
            # submitted crew that could not run read `pending` for good. A cancel that landed
            # first stands, as it does in the registry.
            var dag_status = AGNOSAI_CREW_FAILED;
            var dag_reason = AGNOSAI_CREW_REASON_INVALID_DAG;
            if (agnosai_crew_runner_is_cancelled(r) == 1) {
                dag_status = AGNOSAI_CREW_CANCELLED;
                dag_reason = AGNOSAI_CREW_REASON_CANCELLED;
            }
            _agnosai_crew_emit_completed(r, dag_status, dag_reason, 0,
              _agnosai_crew_elapsed_ms(start_ns));
            store64(out_err, load64(&dag_err));
            return 0;
        }
    } else {
        if (kind == AGNOSAI_PROCESS_HIERARCHICAL) {
            _agnosai_crew_log_a(a, SK_WARN,
              # The oracle's own message, verbatim — see roadmap F2.
              "hierarchical mode not yet implemented, falling back to sequential", 63,  #skip-lint: oracle string
              agnosai_crew_id(spec), 0);
        }
        results = _agnosai_crew_run_sequential(r);
    }

    var wall_ms = _agnosai_crew_elapsed_ms(start_ns);

    # A timeout beats everything, then a cancel; otherwise every task must have
    # completed.
    #
    # ⚠ **A timeout is reported HERE, by the runner (ADR 022).** It used to be
    # computed from whatever partial results existed — COMPLETED when they had
    # all completed, *including when there were none* — and emitted in
    # `crew_completed`, and only afterwards did the orchestrator store FAILED.
    # A watcher read "completed" for a crew the registry called failed. The
    # timeout state is now built here, so the event and the stored state agree by
    # construction. Timeout outranks cancel, as the orchestrator's substitution
    # always has (`_agnosai_orch_finish_err`).
    #
    # The two can coincide only if a cancel lands between the loop head that saw
    # the deadline and this read. Both are read at the same loop heads with the
    # cancel checked first, so no suite can produce it deterministically; the
    # precedence is stated rather than tested.
    var timed_out = agnosai_crew_runner_timed_out(r);
    var status = AGNOSAI_CREW_COMPLETED;
    var reason = 0;
    if (timed_out == 1) {
        status = AGNOSAI_CREW_FAILED;
        reason = AGNOSAI_CREW_REASON_TIMEOUT;
    } elif (agnosai_crew_runner_is_cancelled(r) == 1) {
        status = AGNOSAI_CREW_CANCELLED;
        reason = AGNOSAI_CREW_REASON_CANCELLED;
    } else {
        # ⚠ **A crew whose budget ran out says so (ADR 033)**, when it is why the crew did not
        # complete: a loop head stopped it, or its budget is spent and a task fell short, as a
        # call the budget refused fails its task. A crew that spent its budget on its last call
        # and completed everything completed. A task's own limit is that task's failure, and says
        # so on its result.
        for (var i = 0; i < vec_len(results); i = i + 1) {
            if (agnosai_task_result_status(vec_get(results, i)) != AGNOSAI_STATUS_COMPLETED) {
                status = AGNOSAI_CREW_FAILED;
            }
        }
        # ⚠ A task left with no result did not complete (2.1.6): a DAG whose
        # failure stranded its dependents, or a worker that produced nothing.
        # Counting only the results that exist is the vacuous-`all()` shape — a
        # crew reported COMPLETED for work that never ran.
        if (vec_len(results) < vec_len(tasks)) { status = AGNOSAI_CREW_FAILED; }
        if (status == AGNOSAI_CREW_FAILED) {
            reason = AGNOSAI_CREW_REASON_TASK_FAILED;
            if (agnosai_crew_runner_budget_exceeded(r) == 1 || _agnosai_crew_budget_spent(r) == 1) {
                reason = AGNOSAI_CREW_REASON_BUDGET_EXCEEDED;
            }
        }
    }

    # Balance the active gauge and fold this crew's tasks into the counters.
    # Both run for every terminal status — Completed, Failed and Cancelled — so
    # the gauge cannot drift upward on a cancelled crew. A timed-out crew's
    # results are counted too: the work ran and its tokens were spent, even
    # though the state below discards them.
    agnosai_metrics_record_crew_completed(agnosai_metrics_global());
    _agnosai_crew_record_metrics(results);

    # On a timeout this is the oracle's timeout state — `Failed`, no results, no
    # profile (`orchestrator.rs:204-214` as of tag 2.1.4) — so a caller cannot
    # read a timed-out crew's partial output as if it had finished.
    var state = agnosai_crew_state_new_a(a, agnosai_crew_id(spec), status);
    if (reason != 0) { agnosai_crew_state_with_reason(state, reason); }
    if (timed_out == 0) {
        for (var j = 0; j < vec_len(results); j = j + 1) {
            agnosai_crew_state_with_result_a(a, state, vec_get(results, j));
        }
        agnosai_crew_state_with_profile(state, _agnosai_crew_build_profile(r, results, wall_ms));
    }

    # `task_count` is the STATE's result count, not the run's, so a timed-out
    # crew reports 0 — what the stored state holds. `wall_ms` stays the real one.
    _agnosai_crew_emit_completed(r, status, reason, vec_len(agnosai_crew_state_results(state)),
      wall_ms);

    # ⚠ CANCELLED is an ERROR span, not OK. A collector's error rate is what an
    # operator alerts on, and a cancelled crew did not do what was asked — the
    # oracle's `CrewStatus` keeps the three apart and OTLP has only two, so the
    # fold has to pick a side and picks the honest one. A timed-out crew is
    # FAILED above, so its span is ERROR too — it used to be OK whenever its
    # partial results had all completed. `error.type` keeps the three apart
    # (ADR 023), and the timeout is read from the runner itself, so the span
    # agrees with the orchestrator's FAILED however `status` was reached.
    var err_type = 0;
    if (timed_out == 1) { err_type = "timeout"; }
    elif (status == AGNOSAI_CREW_CANCELLED) { err_type = "cancelled"; }
    elif (reason == AGNOSAI_CREW_REASON_BUDGET_EXCEEDED) { err_type = "budget_exceeded"; }
    elif (status != AGNOSAI_CREW_COMPLETED) { err_type = "task_failed"; }
    _agnosai_crew_span_done(span, self_sc, parent_sc, span_t0, err_type);
    return state;
}

